{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000147153789": {
      "title": "Technical Officer - Simulation and Practical Experience (SPE)",
      "description": "Looking to step away from shift work, while still using your healthcare skills? Join Griffith University as a Technical Officer - Simulation (Nursing and Midwifery) to support the delivery of realistic learning experiences for Nursing and Midwifery students. This hands-on role involves preparing, maintain and supporting clinical teaching laboratories and simulations spaces ensuring equipment, facilities and consumables are ready for student learning. Perfect for someone who enjoys the practical side of healthcare, this is a technical support role rather than a teaching or clinical care position. No night shifts, no weekends. Weekday hours, with shifts between 7am and 6pm, and a positive work-life balance. Here’s how you’ll make it matter: Prepare and maintain clinical teaching laboratories and simulation environments for Nursing and Midwifery students. Provide technical support for practical classes and simulation-based learning activities. Manage equipment, consumables, stock control and procurement to ensure resources are safe, available and fit for purpose. Promote safe and compliant work practices, supporting Work Health and Safety requirements across learning spaces. Work collaboratively with academic and professional staff to deliver high-quality student learning experiences and continuous improvement initiatives. About you What matters most is your commitment to making a positive impact. You’ll bring practical, reliable and service-focused approach, with experience in healthcare, clinical learning or technical support environments. You take pride in creating safe, well-prepared learning spaces and enjoy hands-on work. With strong organisational skills, attention to detail and a proactive mindset, you can manage competing priorities while delivering high-quality outcomes. You will also have: Relevant health qualification, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Experience providing clinical, laboratory, simulation or technical support, ideally in healthcare, tertiary education or clinical skills environments. Demonstrated ability to maintain safe, compliant learning environments, with strong knowledge of Work Health and Safety practices. Strong organisational, communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work collaboratively with diverse stakeholders. Practical experience supporting clinical learning spaces, simulation environments, equipment and resources, with proficiency in Microsoft 365 and a willingness to learn new technologies. Experience supporting Nursing, Midwifery or other Health discipline simulation activities is highly regarded. A current driver’s licence is desirable. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position primarily based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 4 range $76,126 - $80,321per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $89,067 - $93,976 per annum. \n\n Why join Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Anna Marshall, Team Leader - Clinical Skills and Simulation at anna.marshall@griffith.edu.au. For application and recruitment advice, please Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au Closing date:  Wednesday 16th September at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. Griffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000147146579": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Clinical Psychology and Internship Coordinator)",
      "description": "As a   Lecturer (Clinical Psychology and Internship Coordinator) , you will use your skills and experience to advance clinical psychology training and professional practice education and drive meaningful change. Here’s how you’ll make it matter: Lead high quality clinical education through engaging, student centred learning and assessment across postgraduate psychology programs. Coordinate and strengthen all Psychology Clinic placements, ensuring seamless operations, clear communication, and exceptional professional standards. Build excellence in clinical supervision by providing individual, paired, and group supervision that supports safe, ethical, evidence based practice. Collaborate with Clinic and Program leadership to shape future focused training models, assessment processes, and placement pathways. Contribute to key academic activities, including OSCE delivery, postgraduate selection, and advisory committees to uphold program quality and compliance. This is a continuing, full time position primarily based at Griffith University Nathan campus. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Academic Level B range $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $136,979 - $162,667 per annum. \n\n What matters most is your drive to create a brighter future that benefits all. Through our core principles of excellence, ethics and engagement, you’ll thrive on seeing the direct and lasting impact you’ll have on every community and every future. You will also have: A postgraduate qualification in psychology, registration as a psychologist in Australia, and clinical psychology area‑of‑practice endorsement. Eligibility to provide supervision under Psychology Board of Australia requirements. Demonstrated experience supporting postgraduate learning in clinical or professional psychology settings, with the ability to guide students through complex practice‑based challenges. Strong organisational capability, with the judgement and professionalism required to manage sensitive information, competing priorities, and dynamic clinical training environments. Excellent communication and relationship‑building skills, enabling effective collaboration with academic leaders, clinic staff, students, and external partners. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Allison Waters, Head of School - School of Applied Psychology at a.waters@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au . Closing date:  Thursday, 17 September at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. The School of Applied Psychology are a leading education provider offering accredited degrees in psychology, counselling, suicidology and health services management. Our programs emphasise practical skill-based learning and the application of disciple knowledge across diverse professional settings. Graduates are equipped to support individuals facing personal, health, vocational or social challenges and to lead impactful initiatives within the health sector.",
      "department": "School of Applied Psychology"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000147098729": {
      "title": "Integrity & Complaints Officer",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  The Integrity and Complaints Officer provides expert advice and guidance to investigators and specialist teams on procedural integrity across complaints and integrity-related processes. The role supports the timely, consistent, and compliant resolution of complex matters, while contributing to the development and implementation of strategies, solutions, and business process improvements that enhance the management of complaints and associated processes. Key responsibilities Provide frontline advice and intake services for complaints and integrity matters, assessing enquiries and referring issues to appropriate channels. Receive, assess and triage complaints, academic integrity matters, reviews and appeals, conducting initial risk assessments and referrals in line with University policies and legislative requirements. Manage low-risk student matters, including providing advice and supporting early resolution where appropriate. Coordinate complaint administration throughout the case lifecycle, maintaining accurate, confidential records and ensuring compliance with information management requirements. Provide administrative and secretariat support for complaints governance activities, including the University Appeals Committee. Support the annual Declaration of Interests process through system administration, enquiry management, monitoring and reporting. Maintain and improve complaints and integrity frameworks, processes, systems and resources to support efficient and compliant case management. Monitor trends, risks and systemic issues, providing insights and recommendations to support continuous improvement. Develop and maintain complaints and integrity guidance, content and training resources. Foster collaborative relationships across the University to support effective case management and promote a culture of integrity. About you Completion of a relevant tertiary qualification or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. What we can offer \nThis is a full time, continuing opportunity primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 6 – Base range - $94,966.18 - $101,295.51 + 17% superannuation. Full package range - $111,110.42 - $118,515.72. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Thursday 17th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "General Counsel"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146886199": {
      "title": "Dental Assistant",
      "description": "Griffith Health Clinics provide high-quality primary healthcare services while supporting the education and training of the next generation of health professionals. Based at Warwick, we are seeking a Dental Assistant to support students and staff in delivering exceptional patient care. Key responsibilities for this role will include: Assist staff and students across a broad range of dental treatments and procedures. Prepare patients for treatment and support clinic operations, working autonomously and using initiative to manage day-to-day responsibilities effectively. Maintain clinical hygiene and adhere to infection control protocols. Provide administrative and reception support. Uphold university values and comply with policies, health and safety requirements, and professional About you \nTo be successful in this role, you will hold: An Australian Dental Assistant qualification or recognised equivalent is highly desirable. Current CPR/First Aid Certification from a recognised provider. Current Queensland Blue Card (or willingness to obtain). In addition to the above, you will demonstrate: Excellent teamwork and communication skills. Ability to work under supervision and contribute to efficient clinic operations. Strong administrative skills with attention to detail. A commitment to patient care and student learning. What we can offer \nThis is a full-time, fixed-term position until October 2027, based primarily at Griffith University’s Warwick location. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 4 range $76,126 - $80,321 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $89,067 - $93,976 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description )  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact the Dental Clinic Manager at  dentalclinicmanager@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Wednesday, 16 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146885524": {
      "title": "Privacy Compliance Manager",
      "description": "About the Opportunity The Privacy Compliance Manager leads the University’s privacy compliance program through consultative and constructive interaction with element leadership, managers, and liaisons. Success depends on a risk-based approach to define priorities, a commitment to foster strong relationships, open communication towards stakeholders, and dedication to cultivating behaviours and actions that uphold and reinforce integrity, accountability and transparency Key Responsibilities Oversee and manage Right to Information (RTI), administrative access, and related privacy complaints. Conduct and supervise investigations into RTI, administrative access matters and privacy complaints; identify and resolve systemic privacy issues in collaboration with University staff. Lead the development and delivery of privacy education, training, and awareness programs. Monitor legislative changes and update University policies, procedures, and templates accordingly. Support the University’s privacy compliance program and assist the Privacy Officer with RTI, administrative access and complaint matters. Maintain and report statistics on RTI, administrative access and privacy complaints to internal and external stakeholders. Liaise with regulatory bodies during compliance reviews and investigations. Develop and implement a risk-based privacy compliance program aligned with University strategy and legal requirements. Guide continuous improvement of the University's privacy compliance framework in collaboration with stakeholders. Advise senior leadership on privacy obligations, including data mapping, risk assessments and privacy impact assessments. Support internal audits and advise privacy champions across the University. Promote accountability by advising on and monitoring corrective actions. Represent privacy interests in University committees and working groups to enhance privacy practices. Provide regular updates and reports to senior management and the Audit & Risk Committee on compliance progress. About you The occupant of this position will hold relevant postgraduate qualifications (preferably legal qualifications) with at least five (5) years-experience in a similar role or have an equivalent level of expertise obtained from a combination of experience, training, professional accreditation and qualification. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and may be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer \n \nThis is a full time, continuing opportunity primarily based Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 10 - Base salary of $147,057.74 + 17% super. Full package $172,057.55. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description.  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acqusition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 20th September 2026  at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "General Counsel"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146877759": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship: Responsible Infrastructure for Agentic AI Systems",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to investigate the responsible design, evaluation, and governance of infrastructure for agentic AI systems .  The primary focus of this role is to develop methods that improve the reliability, accountability, and resilience of shared runtime components used by autonomous and multi-agent AI systems .  This project includes research on agent memory, shared state, tool-use workflows, retrieval-augmented generation, context management, and runtime monitoring mechanisms .  Success in this role requires collaboration with academic researchers, industry partners, and interdisciplinary stakeholders working on trustworthy AI, software systems, and emerging digital infrastructure. This project is embedded in an ARC Linkage Project, offering the candidate opportunities to work with academic and industry partners on responsible infrastructure for next-generation agentic AI systems. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful within this role, you will be able to demonstrate strong analytical, programming, and problem-solving skills, with the capacity to conduct independent research in AI systems, software infrastructure, and responsible technology development. You will have evidence of a passion for agentic AI, trustworthy AI, multi-agent systems, and intelligent software infrastructure, with a strong desire to undertake research in [responsible infrastructure for agentic AI systems. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and international applicants. Applicants must have completed a Bachelor’s degree with honours equivalent to  first-class honours , or a Master’s degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a Bachelor honours degree, or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Policy . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program, refer  here . Please note : Applicants should have a strong background in Information Technology, Computer Science, Mathematics, Artificial Intelligence, or a closely related discipline. Applicants should have a strong academic record, normally with a GPA greater than 85/100 or equivalent. Applicants with prior research experience, research project involvement, peer-reviewed publications, or demonstrated experience in AI, machine learning, software systems, multi-agent systems, or related areas will be highly regarded. International applicants should ensure that English Language Proficiency requirements for the program are met before formally applying. Applicants to research programs will need to show they meet: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR A score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 OR A score of no less than 3+ in each skill of the ISLPR (conducted by ISLPR Language Services only) OR A minimum overall score of 176 (no score less than 169) on C1 Advanced  or  C2 Proficiency OR An overall score of 58 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 50 (applies to tests taken prior to 7 August 2025) OR An overall score of 55 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 47 (applies to tests taken on or after 7 August 2025) OR An overall score of 70 in the LanguageCert Academic OR An overall score of 59 in the Michigan English Test (MET) . \n\n About the scholarship The 2026 Griffith University Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $35,656 (indexed) for a period of up to three years of full-time study. Please see the  GUPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. A successful International applicant will also be awarded a Griffith University International Postgraduate Research Scholarship to cover tuition fees for up to three years full time study. Please see the  GUIPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  leo.zhang@griffith.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Brief research proposal, maximum 2 pages Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is: Friday 02 October 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nProfessor Leo Zhang \nPhone: (07) 5552 8834 \nEmail:   leo.zhang@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The project will be undertaken within the School of Information and Communication Technology at Griffith University, in connection with the TrustAGI research lab. The lab conducts research on trustworthy and responsible artificial intelligence, with interests spanning agentic AI systems, multi-agent coordination, AI governance, software systems, and emerging digital infrastructure. This opportunity will be primarily based at Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146861784": {
      "title": "Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services",
      "description": "Join our supportive and dedicated Researcher Services team!  As a Discipline Librarian reporting to the Coordinator Researcher Services, you will:  Foster collaborative relationships across the Academic Engagement Services team, the wider Library portfolio, and with key university entities including the Office for Research, eResearch, and Griffith Graduate Research School. Work closely with your colleagues and with other teams across the Library to provide advice to researchers and HDR candidates on topics such as working with literature, scholarly publishing, researcher profiles and metrics and working with data.   Develop and evaluate effectiveness of researcher training workshops and eLearning resources.  Provide discipline-relevant scholarly information via consultation services with researchers and HDR candidates.   The role involves interacting face-to-face and virtually with academic staff and students. As such, you will be innovative, open-minded, approachable, collaborative, and have excellent communication, leadership and teamwork skills This is an exciting opportunity to develop your skills within a strong ‘One Library’ culture.    About you   Qualifications in librarianship, information management or equivalent. Experience in an academic or research library, and an understanding of the evolving research environment within universities. Demonstrated experience in developing and delivering workshops and delivering online consultations is desirable. Demonstrated knowledge of supporting systematic-style literature reviews, scholarly publishing, researcher profiles and metrics. Experience with use of research technologies and research literature focused AI tools. Good communication skills with strong client support, teaching, eLearning resource creation, and problem-solving skills.  You will be comfortable operating in a rapidly changing environment. Being flexible, open-minded, approachable, collaborative, well-organised and having a strong teamwork ethic is vital. You will be committed to a focus on continuous improvement.   What we can offer     Two continuing full-time Discipline Librarian roles:   Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services (Business)  Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services (Science)  The full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 7: $104,504 – $113,219 p.a. + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be $122,270 – $132,466 per annum. Your primary campus location for: Researcher Services (Business) will be based at Brisbane South (Nathan) Researcher Services (Science) at Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations.   Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.  \n\n   \n\n At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information, please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . Why Griffith?  As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We support the professional and personal development of all our employees and invest in the skills of our people. We offer:  mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment.  salary packaging options and corporate health discounts.  generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading.  support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme. How to apply  Please provide the following:  A cover letter   Addressing your suitability for the position (not more than 2 pages)  Refer to the 'About you' section above and the Position Description   Indicate your preference for the Business or Sciences Discipline Librarian and primary campus location - Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast.   Your current CV/Resume  Applicants must have Australian working rights for the duration of this contract. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. Sponsorship is not offered for this position.  For further information specifically about this role , please contact Ryan Weymouth, Coordinator, Researcher Services at r.weymouth@griffith.edu.au or via Teams.  For application or recruitment support please contact Kate Watson - Talent Acquisition Partner via email: kate.watson@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 6th September 2026 AEST at 11:59 pm. All applications must be submitted online.   \n\n This is an exciting opportunity to join Griffith Library's Academic Engagement Services team.  You will help Griffith researchers and Higher Degree Research (HDR) candidates to excel through the provision of integrated, high-impact researcher support services.  We are seeking two dynamic and proactive Discipline Librarians to join our Researcher Services team to support researchers and HDR candidates respectively from Griffith’s Business School and Griffith’s Sciences Group.",
      "department": "Library"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146615724": {
      "title": "Technical Officer ",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  We are seeking a skilled Technical Officer to support the delivery of teaching and research outcomes within our mechanical workshop facilities. Working under the direction of the Manager or Lead Technical Officer, you will manufacture, maintain and repair specialised equipment, provide technical guidance to staff and students, and ensure workshop operations are safe, efficient and compliant. This is an excellent opportunity to apply your technical expertise in a collaborative environment that supports innovation, learning and research excellence. Key responsibilities Support teaching, research and consultancy activities through the manufacture, modification, maintenance and repair of mechanical equipment and components. Interpret technical drawings and specifications to fabricate, machine and weld specialised equipment. Provide technical advice and hands-on support to staff and students in the design and development of equipment and prototypes. Diagnose faults and perform maintenance and repairs on workshop and University equipment. Deliver training and guidance on the safe use of workshop tools, machinery and processes. Maintain workshop records, inventory and procurement activities to ensure efficient operations. Contribute to a safe, compliant and continuously improving workshop environment. Provide excellent customer service and collaborate effectively with colleagues across Technical Services and the Sciences Group. Undertake other duties consistent with the level and responsibilities of the role. About you The occupant of this position will hold a recognised trade qualification in fitting and turning, mechanical engineering trades, metal fabrication, or a related discipline, together with demonstrated experience in the operation of workshop machinery and fabrication equipment, including lathes, milling machines, saws, grinders, presses and welding equipment. Alternatively, an equivalent combination of relevant qualifications, training and practical experience may be considered. What we can offer \nThis is a full time continuing opportunity primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 5 – Base range - $82,415.66 - $92,456.55 + 17% super. Full package range - $96,426.31 - $108,174.16. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 20th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. (please note advertising for this role has been extended, all previous applicants are still being considered) \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Griffith Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146616279": {
      "title": "Mechanical Workshop Technical Lead",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  Lead the Mechanical Workshop team in delivering high-quality mechanical assembly, fabrication, welding, and machining services that support research, teaching, and external projects. Using a range of workshop equipment, including lathes, milling machines, water-jet cutters, and welding equipment, you will provide technical expertise, coordinate resources and priorities, and work closely with the Workshop Manager to ensure successful project delivery. Key responsibilities Provide technical guidance to the Workshops Manager on equipment, maintenance, training, and operational matters. Plan and deliver fabrication, repair, and engineering projects, from procurement through to completion. Oversee daily workshop operations, ensuring safe, efficient, and high-quality service delivery. Work with clients to define project requirements, timelines, and outcomes. Support the scheduling, prioritisation, and allocation of workshop resources. Lead and mentor the Mechanical Workshop team, maintaining quality standards and resolving technical issues. Drive continuous improvement initiatives to enhance workshop performance and efficiency. Promote a safe, professional, and well-organised workshop environment. About you The occupant of this position will hold relevant tertiary qualifications and subsequent relevant experience or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Demonstrated competency in tig welding skill of both aluminium and stainless steel. This role also involves working with a range of materials, including metals, plastics, timber, and composites, and the operation of workshop equipment used for manufacturing, machining, fabrication, assembly, and prototyping activities. Experience across these broader technical functions, particularly in the operation of CNC manufacturing equipment and associated technologies, would be highly regarded. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing position primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 7 – base range $104,504.60 - $113,219.43 + 17% superannuation. Full package range - $122,270.38 - $132,466.72. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 20th September at 11:59pm.  All applications must be submitted online. \n \n (please note advertising for this role has been extended, all previous applicants are still being considered) \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Griffith Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146615239": {
      "title": "Centre Manager, Griffith Head and Neck Centre",
      "description": "About the opportunity  \nThe Griffith Head and Neck Centre is a newly established joint clinical academic centre of Griffith University and Gold Coast Hospital and Health Service, supported during its founding period by the Garnett Passe and Rodney Williams Memorial Foundation. The Centre brings together clinicians, researchers, educators, scientists, engineers, consumers and industry partners to improve care for people with disorders of the head and neck.   We are seeking an experienced and entrepreneurial Centre Manager to help build the Centre during its formative years. Working closely with the founding Director, Professor Jason Fleming, you will play a central role in establishing the Centre’s operational foundations while helping shape its long-term growth, partnerships and sustainability. This is a unique and exciting opportunity to contribute to the development of a new clinical academic centre with genuine scope to influence its direction from the outset. This is not a research position, nor is it a traditional administrative role. Dedicated administrative support is provided separately, allowing the Centre Manager to focus on strategic operations, partnership development, governance, business development and program delivery.   About the role \nAs Centre Manager you will: Support delivery of the Centre’s strategic priorities, business planning and governance; Develop partnerships with industry, philanthropy, government and academic collaborators; Coordinate the Centre’s programs, initiatives and major projects; Oversee financial planning, reporting and performance monitoring; Build productive relationships across Griffith University, Gold Coast Hospital and Health Service and external partners; and Contribute to the long-term growth and sustainability of the Centre. This is a part-time (0.5 FTE), fixed-term appointment for five years based at Griffith University’s Gold Coast location. As Griffith University is a multi-campus institution you may be required to work at other campuses or other sites on occasion. The position is classified at professional staff HEW 8 level, and the step at which the successful candidate is appointed will depend on their educational and professional background. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 8 range $117,579 - $132,273 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $137,567 - $154,759 per annum. Why join us? \nThis is a rare opportunity to help establish a new multidisciplinary clinical academic centre from its inception. You will work directly with senior academic, clinical and external partners while contributing to research, education and innovation with the potential to improve patient care across Queensland and beyond. You’ll join a supportive team within the School of Medicine and Dentistry and Griffith Health and enjoy the flexibility, professional development opportunities and employment benefits offered by Griffith University. \n\n We are seeking applications from experienced professionals who enjoy building organisations and working across strategy, partnerships and operations. The successful candidate will bring: Experience in strategic operations, program management, research development, partnerships or business development within a university, health, research or not-for-profit environment; Demonstrated ability to develop productive stakeholder relationships across complex organisations; Strong organisational, financial and project management skills; Excellent written and verbal communication; Sound judgement, initiative and the ability to work independently within a newly established environment; and A collaborative approach with a genuine interest in supporting research, education and innovation. Experience working within universities, health services, medical research institutes, or advancement environments will be highly regarded. We are seeking candidates who possess a relevant degree with subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Further eligibility requirements are contained in the Position Description which is accessible below.  \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission). Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Jason Fleming via email at: j.fleming@griffith.edu.au . For further application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner on  j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Monday, 21 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. The  School of Medicine and Dentistry  strives to prepare the next generation of professionals and practitioners to tackle health and healthcare challenges in an ever-changing world. As a community, we are committed to building better health outcomes for all by delivering high-quality education, impactful research and engagement with and for the communities we serve. As articulated in our Strategic Plan, we commit to the University's values and, importantly, to being a School for Country, place, communities and the people we serve. Through our learning and teaching, we strive to produce graduates who are confident, competent and resilient to be agents for change.  We are committed to recruiting and retaining the best possible staff; staff who support our mission and vision. The School of Medicine and Dentistry shares the Health Group and broader University's commitment to excellence, ethical behaviour, and engagement. The Health Group Charter articulates the core values and behaviours we expect in our workforce: inclusion, integrity, performance, respect, and collaboration.  These values and behaviours are embedded in the School's annual Academic Employee Career Development (AECD) / Professional Staff Career Development (PSCD) process and frame staff performance planning and review conversations.",
      "department": "School of Medicine and Dentistry"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146614709": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Critical Care Nursing",
      "description": "Are you ready to shape the next generation of Critical Care Nurses and lead transformative research at a global scale?  Griffith University and Princess Alexandra Hospital are seeking a visionary leader to drive innovation, education, and research in Critical Care Nursing through a prestigious joint appointment.  We invite applications from outstanding critical care nursing leaders for a joint appointment as Associate Professor of Critical Care Nursing (Level D).  This prestigious role will suit a highly regarded academic and clinician who is passionate about advancing critical care nursing research, education and evidence-based practice through strong clinical- academic collaboration.  This is a full time, fixed term appointment for five (5) years based in Brisbane, split between Griffith University Brisbane South Nathan and Princess Alexandra Hospital. It is expected that the successful candidate will be available to attend both sites in person as a normal requirement of the role.  Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Academic Level D range $172,686 - $190,248 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $202,043 - $222,590 per annum. \n\n To be successful in this role, you will have excellent communication and interpersonal skills with a demonstrated and extensive commitment to higher education and nursing. In addition, the successful applicant will demonstrate:  Possession of a doctoral qualification or equivalent professional experience in nursing or a relevant field.  Registration with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (Ahpra) as a registered nurse which is to be active and maintained for the duration of the appointment.   An extensive track-record in the ability to attract external competitive research funding, to supervise research students and to build and lead multidisciplinary research teams in Critical Care Nursing.  \n\n How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) \n Important Information for Applicants  Applicants must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Visa sponsorship is not available.  The successful candidate may be required to undergo a criminal history check.  Evidence of vaccination (or the ability to be vaccinated) against the following is required: Measles, Mumps, Rubella, Varicella, Pertussis, and Hepatitis B.  Tuberculosis screening may be required to determine vaccination needs.  Any serious disciplinary history must be disclosed.  For further information about this role, please contact Professor Ann Bonner – Head of School, School of Nursing and Midwifery on  hos.sonm@griffith.edu.au .  For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au             Closing date:   Sunday 6th September at 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Griffith University is a progressive, values-led university dedicated to learning, leadership, and innovation. This is your chance to help us achieve our vision to transform lives and add to human knowledge and understanding in a way that creates a future that benefits all.  The  Griffith School of Nursing and Midwifery  is internationally recognised for excellence in education and research. It is consistently ranked among the world’s leading nursing and midwifery schools (#1 in Australia, #3 in the world; 2025 ShanghaiRanking by Academic Subjects). We are committed to advancing practice, policy and research that improves health outcomes for all.  Princess Alexandra Hospital, Metro South Health    \nPrincess Alexandra Hospital (PAH) is a tertiary health care centre, providing care in all major adult specialties to patients throughout Queensland and Australia, except for obstetrics. We are one of Australia’s leading academic and research health centres, providing Clinical Service Capability Framework (CSCF) Level 6 services, with approximately 1,000 beds. PAH is nationally recognised for expertise in trauma management, and we are a major transplantation centre for livers, kidneys, bone, cartilage, and corneas. We also have responsibility for a number of state-wide services including Queensland Liver Transplant Service, Queensland Tissue Bank, Queensland Spinal Cord Injury Service. The ICU is undergoing a major expansion.  The PAH fulfils a significant teaching and research role, undertaking research and educational programs with affiliated universities and institutes. The expanding research profile is characterised by its full spectrum from basic science to clinical care",
      "department": "School of Nursing and Midwifery"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146393550": {
      "title": "Head of Strategic Procurement",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  The Head of Strategic Procurement is a key leadership role within the Finance team, responsible for driving the University's procurement strategy and delivering a high-performing, value-focused procurement function. Leading a team of procurement professionals, the role will enhance procurement governance, streamline processes, and improve the stakeholder experience while ensuring commercially sound, transparent, and compliant procurement outcomes. Operating within a centre-led, decentralised procurement model, the Head of Strategic Procurement will provide expert advice to senior stakeholders, drive procurement excellence and efficiencies, and foster a strong customer-focused approach. The role also ensures the University meets its regulatory, financial, ethical, sustainability, modern slavery, risk, and responsible procurement obligations, supporting the achievement of the University's broader strategic objectives. Key Responsibilities Lead the University’s procurement function, delivering efficient, value-driven procurement services aligned to strategic and operational priorities. Drive procurement governance, risk management, compliance, and assurance frameworks to support transparent and accountable decision-making. Develop and implement procurement strategies, category management approaches, and contract management practices that maximise value, strengthen supplier performance, and reduce complexity. Simplify procurement processes and systems to enhance stakeholder experience, improve efficiency, and provide responsive support. Provide expert procurement, commercial, and governance advice to senior leaders and key stakeholders. Manage the procurement pipeline, ensuring effective prioritisation, planning, and delivery of strategic procurement activities. Build strong stakeholder and supplier relationships to deliver practical solutions and sustainable commercial outcomes. Lead, develop, and empower a high-performing procurement team, fostering accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Promote innovation, digital enablement, ethical procurement practices, and compliance with relevant legislation, policies, and governance requirements. Represent the University in engagement with government, suppliers, industry networks, and external partners. About you Extensive leadership experience in strategic procurement, sourcing, contract management, and supplier governance within complex organisations. Proven ability to lead a centre-led procurement function, delivering strong governance, stakeholder outcomes, and measurable value. Strong commercial acumen with the ability to use data and insights to inform strategy and decision-making. Excellent stakeholder engagement, influencing, and negotiation skills, with success managing complex commercial matters. Sound knowledge of procurement governance, risk, compliance, probity, and contract management. Commitment to sustainable, ethical, and responsible procurement practices. Demonstrated success leading teams, driving change, and building a high-performance culture. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and may be subject to a National criminal history check. Sponsorship is not available for this role.  What we can offer \nThis is a full time, fixed term opportunity for 5 years primarily based Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary A competitive salary will be negotiated with the preferred candidate. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 13th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. We kindly ask recruitment agencies not to send unsolicited CVs or approach members of our hiring team regarding this role, as the recruitment process is being managed internally. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Finance"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146393660": {
      "title": "Head of Financial Control",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  As a key member of the Finance Senior Leadership Team, this role provides strategic leadership across financial reporting, financial control, treasury, tax, financial assurance, and research accounting functions. Reporting to the CFO, the Head of Financial Control delivers trusted financial advice to senior executives, supports informed decision-making, and ensures strong governance, compliance, and financial stewardship across the University. Leading a diverse team, the role is responsible for statutory financial reporting, treasury management, including a significant investment portfolio, and maintaining a robust financial control environment. The position also drives continuous improvement, strengthens financial systems and processes, and fosters a high-performance culture that supports the University's strategic objectives. As a critical leadership role, the incumbent plays a key part in safeguarding the University's fraud and corruption control framework, ensuring effective governance, risk management, and financial integrity across the institution. Key Responsibilities Lead and develop high-performing teams across Financial Management, Treasury, Tax, Financial Assurance, and Research Accounting. Foster a collaborative, customer-focused culture that drives excellence, innovation, and continuous improvement. Provide strategic leadership, coaching, and direction while building strong relationships across the University. Oversee the University's financial control framework, ensuring compliance with accounting standards, legislation, and internal policies. Lead statutory financial reporting, audit management, financial assurance activities, and governance reporting. Maintain the integrity, accuracy, and reliability of the University's financial systems, processes, and reporting. Partner with senior executives to provide expert advice on complex financial, accounting, treasury, tax, and commercial matters. Lead treasury operations, including investment, cashflow, borrowing, and balance sheet management. Manage key external relationships, including auditors, banking and investment partners, and professional advisors. Support strategic initiatives and special projects that contribute to the University's long-term success. Strengthen financial risk management, fraud prevention, and assurance frameworks across the University. Ensure a robust control environment that supports sound decision-making, accountability, and financial sustainability. About you The occupant of this position will hold relevant tertiary qualifications, preferably at postgraduate level, and substantial relevant work experience.  Membership of a professional accredited accounting organisation such as CA,or CPA  is mandatory. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and may be subject to a National criminal history check. Sponsorship is not available for this role.  What we can offer \nThis is a full time, fixed term opportunity for 5 years primarily based Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary A competitive salary will be negotiated with the preferred candidate. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 13th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. We kindly ask recruitment agencies not to send unsolicited CVs or approach members of our hiring team regarding this role, as the recruitment process is being managed internally. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Finance"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146393489": {
      "title": "Director, Griffith Asia Institute",
      "description": "As Director, Griffith Asia Institute, you will shape the Institute’s next chapter—setting its strategic direction, leading its globally connected research community and advancing its ambition for Asia and the Pacific to lead the world in sustainable development. You will champion interdisciplinary research and expand GAI’s influence in policy, practice and public debate. Reporting to the Dean (Research), you will align GAI’s priorities with the research and engagement ambitions of Griffith Business School and the wider University. You will lead and support a high-performing community of researchers and professional staff, oversee governance and resources, and grow and diversify external funding, research, education and capacity-building opportunities. As a visible ambassador for GAI, you will deepen strategic relationships with universities, governments, industry, donors and communities across Asia and the Pacific. You will translate the Institute’s regional expertise into meaningful impact and position Griffith as a trusted partner on the issues shaping the region’s future. About you You are an internationally respected academic leader with the vision and collaborative style to guide GAI’s next phase. You bring a doctoral qualification and significant national and international standing, deep Asia-Pacific expertise, a strong record of research and thought leadership, and experience leading a prominent institute, centre or complex academic enterprise. Associate Professor (Level D) and Professor (Level E) are encouraged to apply. You will demonstrate: Inspiring and inclusive leadership, with a record of developing people and building high-performing teams. Strong regional networks and the cultural capability to build trusted partnerships across sectors and borders. Success translating interdisciplinary research into policy, practice and public debate. A proven ability to secure and diversify external funding and income. Sound strategic, governance and resource-management capability. What we can offer This is a fixed-term (for five years), full-time position and you will be primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. Please note that Griffith Business School at Brisbane South (Nathan) will be relocating to Griffith CBD, with an intended relocation date of early 2027. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations   \n\n A doctoral qualification in a discipline related to the Institute \n\n Why Griffith? At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . How to apply \nPlease submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the Job Description/Key Accountabilities above and the  position description ). Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details. Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Visa sponsorship may be available to the successful candidate. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Chris Fleming, Dean Research, Griffith Business School, via email: chris.fleming@griffith.edu.au. For further application and recruitment advice, please contact Kate Watson, Talent Acquisition Partner via email: kate.watson@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Thursday 1st October,  at 11:59 pm AEST All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 2 – 3 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. Griffith Asia Institute (GAI) is an internationally recognised research centre within Griffith Business School. GAI reflects Griffith University's enduring commitment to the study and engagement of nations in Asia and the Pacific. Propelling Asia and the Pacific to become global leaders in sustainable development. Building on this strong foundation, GAI brings world-class expertise to the defining challenges and opportunities shaping Asia and the Pacific. Through ambitious interdisciplinary research, influential regional partnerships and evidence-based engagement, the Institute connects ideas with action across government, business, academia and communities. For an incoming Director, this is a rare opportunity to lead an established, high-impact institute and strengthen its voice, reach and influence at a pivotal time for the region.",
      "department": "Griffith Business School"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146120279": {
      "title": "Head of Department, Department of Management",
      "description": "As Head of Department, Department of Management, you will lead a thriving academic department to strengthen impactful research, deepen industry connections, and pursue outstanding student outcomes. Reporting to the Dean (Academic), you will provide strategic direction and leadership across teaching, research, engagement and people management, ensuring the ongoing success and growth. You will cultivate an environment that encourages innovation, collaboration and academic achievement, while driving initiatives that strengthen the Department of Management’s reputation, performance and contribution to Griffith University’s strategic objectives. About you As an accomplished academic leader in Management or a related field, you have a demonstrated history of advancing excellence in teaching, research and external engagement. You possess the strategic insight, leadership expertise and collaborative mindset required to guide a multidisciplinary academic department and inspire outstanding outcomes across people, programs and partnerships. A skilled and influential communicator, you establish strong relationships with academic colleagues, students, industry stakeholders, professional associations and community partners. You bring experience leading complex teams and overseeing budgets and resources, and you are committed to fostering a supportive, high-performing culture in a dynamic and evolving environment. To be successful in this role, you will also possess: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent accreditation and standing A strong record of achievement in learning and teaching, research and/or industry engagement Proven experience managing people, performance, budgets and resources to achieve strategic objectives The ability to drive innovation, foster collaboration and lead organisational change Excellent communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills A commitment to academic excellence, integrity, equity, diversity and inclusion Most importantly, you will be a values-led leader who can inspire others, champion continuous improvement and contribute to Griffith University's reputation as a leading institution for education, research and community impact. What we can offer The successful candidate will be appointed as Head of Department, Department Management on an initial fixed-term, five-year senior contract and will hold an ongoing, continuing substantive appointment at a Professorial level. You may be primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. Please note that Griffith Business School at Brisbane South (Nathan) will be relocating to Griffith CBD, with an intended relocation date of February 2027.  As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nA competitive salary will be negotiated with the successful candidate. \n\n As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. You will enjoy: Generous superannuation and leave provisions. Access to health and wellbeing programs, fitness facilities and employee assistance services. How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information, please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Visa sponsorship may be available to the successful candidate. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Martin Berka, Dean (Academic), Griffith Business School via gbsdeanacademic@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Kate Watson, Talent Acquisition Partner via kate.watson@griffith.edu.au Closing date:  Wednesday 30th September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 2 – 3 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. The Department of Management brings together leading expertise in business innovation, entrepreneurship, health services management, employment relations and human resources, delivering a globally recognised and industry-relevant education. Emphasising sustainability, adaptability, diversity and ethics, we prepare students to lead and succeed in evolving business environments across the Asia-Pacific region and beyond. Recognised as Australia's leading research institution in Strategic Management ( The Australian 2026 Research Magazine ), the Department influences policy, business practice and regulation while equipping graduates to address complex local and global challenges. Through strong industry engagement and innovative teaching, we develop the skills, knowledge and connections needed to create lasting business impact. In addition, scheduled to open in the not-too-distant future, our new location,  our heritage-listed Treasury Building in Brisbane’s CBD, represents an exciting, fresh chapter for the University and our students.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146119619": {
      "title": "Head of Department, Tourism and Marketing",
      "description": "As Head of Department, Tourism and Marketing, you will provide strategic leadership to a high-performing academic community renowned for its impact-driven research, industry partnerships and commitment to student success. Reporting to the Dean (Academic), you will shape the direction of the Department, driving excellence across teaching, research, external engagement and people leadership. This is a unique opportunity to influence the future of two dynamic and globally connected disciplines. You will foster a culture of innovation, collaboration and academic excellence while leading strategic initiatives that enhance the Department's reputation, performance and contribution to Griffith University's vision. Working closely with colleagues across the Griffith Business School and the wider University, you will champion high-quality learning experiences, support research growth and translation, strengthen industry and community partnerships, and ensure the effective stewardship of resources to achieve ambitious strategic goals. About you \nAs a respected academic leader in a Marketing or Tourism (or related) discipline with a strong track record of fostering excellence in teaching, research and external engagement, you bring the strategic vision, leadership capability and collaborative approach needed to lead a diverse academic department and inspire high performance across people, programs and partnerships. You are an effective relationship builder, able to engage confidently with colleagues, students, industry partners, professional bodies and the broader community. With experience leading complex teams and managing significant resources, you thrive in a dynamic environment and are passionate about creating a positive, inclusive and high-performing culture. To be successful in this role, you will possess: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent accreditation and standing Demonstrated leadership experience within higher education or a similarly complex organisation A strong record of achievement in learning and teaching, research and/or industry engagement Proven experience managing people, budgets and resources to achieve strategic objectives The ability to drive innovation, foster collaboration and lead organisational change Excellent communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills A commitment to academic excellence, integrity, equity, diversity and inclusion Most importantly, you will be a values-led leader who can inspire others, champion continuous improvement and contribute to Griffith University's reputation as a leading institution for education, research and community impact. What we can offer The successful candidate will be appointed as Head of Department, Department of Tourism and Marketing on an initial fixed-term, five-year senior contract and will hold an ongoing, continuing substantive appointment at a Professorial level. You may be primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. Please note that Griffith Business School at Brisbane South (Nathan) will be relocating to Griffith CBD, with an intended relocation date of February 2027.  As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nA competitive salary will be negotiated with the successful candidate. \n\n At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information, please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Visa sponsorship may be available to the successful candidate. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Martin Berka, Dean (Academic), Griffith Business School via gbsdeanacademic@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Kate Watson, Talent Acquisition Partner via kate.watson@griffith.edu.au Closing date:  Wednesday 30th September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 2 – 3 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. Our Department of Tourism and Marketing combines the expertise of world-renowned academic staff to deliver an unmatched educational experience. Our internationally recognised degrees bring together industry-relevant content, cutting-edge research and strong partnerships. We are recognized as Australia's top research institution in Marketing as well as Hospitality and Tourism ( 2026 Australian Research Magazine ) and ranked 6th globally and 1st in Australia for Hospitality and Tourism in the  ShanghaiRanking (GRAS), 2025. In addition, scheduled to open in the not-too-distant future, our new location,  our heritage-listed Treasury Building in Brisbane’s CBD, represents an exciting, fresh chapter for the University and our students.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146111059": {
      "title": "Timetabling Services Officer",
      "description": "Functioning within Student Life, the Exams and Timetabling team specifically provides operational and administrative support and services to academic elements and students for all class timetabling, examination management, grade publication and student progression processes as well as assessment applications and room bookings. Working with the Timetabling Services team, you’ll be responsible for class timetabling for a group of schools, as well as supporting full class management and room bookings. Key Responsibilities Build and maintain strong, trusted relationships with academic and professional staff Deliver accurate class timetabling and room booking outcomes within agreed timeframes Independently resolve complex scheduling issues using specialised timetabling systems Develop optimal scheduling solutions aligned with University policies and priorities Ensure efficient and effective use of teaching spaces and facilities Identify, recommend, and implement opportunities to streamline, improve, and automate processes Contribute to an enhanced student and staff experience through service excellence About you To be successful in the role you will have the demonstrated ability to: Experience in timetabling, scheduling, space management, or a similar coordination role Demonstrated ability to manage complex data and competing priorities with high attention to detail Proven stakeholder engagement skills, with confidence working with academic staff Strong analytical and problem‑solving capability, including system‑based troubleshooting Experience using complex systems and databases (timetabling or scheduling systems highly regarded) Ability to work autonomously while contributing effectively to a collaborative team Continuous improvement mindset with a focus on efficiency and service quality What We Can Offer This is a fixed-term (12 months), full-time position primarily based at our Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site University, you will be required to work across other locations. Salary Range The full-time equivalent base salary will be in the range HEW Level 6: $94,966 – $101,295 p.a. + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range of  $111,110 – $118,515 per annum. \n\n Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter (no longer than 2 pages) outlining your suitability for the role referring to the key responsibilities in the  Position Description Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details. Applicants must have Australian working rights and successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Caroline Carlish, Manager, Timetabling Services, via email c.carlish@griffith.edu.au Closing date:  Sunday 13th September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 3 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Fixed Term (12 months), Full-time opportunity within Student and Academic Services Brisbane South (Nathan) based",
      "department": "Student Life"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146095519": {
      "title": "School Manager - School of Nursing and Midwifery",
      "description": "The School of Nursing and Midwifery is currently recruiting a passionate and dedicated School Manager to join the team of senior administrative staff within the Health Group. This role will ensure the quality and consistency of high service delivery as well as support positive teaching and student experiences across a large school.  \n  \nThe School Manager is the primary advisor to the Head of School and School Executive on all matters pertaining to the University, Group and School policies, operations, and business processes.  Key responsibilities include:  Providing high level advice and support to the Head of School and other senior staff.  Support with planning, budgeting, resource management and other operational matters along with external engagement activities.  Assist with academic work allocation.  Lead business improvement and change processes.  Manage and lead School professional staff.  The position actively participates as a member of the School Executive and plays a key role in contributing to the strategic and operational objectives of the School and Group.  About you \nTo be successful in this position, you will hold relevant tertiary qualifications and evidence of extensive management experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training.  In addition to the above, you will demonstrate:  A strategic thinker, with a track record of developing and maintaining strong relationships.  A collaborative team member with proven expertise in leading an administrative team in a high-pressure environment.  Excellent written and verbal communication skills with experience in the preparation of briefings, information papers, budget and other reports.  Project management and/or business process improvement experience, with an ability to partner with colleagues to overcome complex matters in short timeframes.  Knowledge and/or experience in the contemporary challenges facing the higher education sector.  Sound understanding of financial matters including high level reporting.  What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position primarily based at Griffith University’s Gold Coast campus. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. HEW level 9 salary range $137,165 - $142,070 per annum, plus 17% superannuation.  \n\n Why join Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description )  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Ann Bonner ,  Head of School  on  hos.sonm@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au . Closing date:  11:59pm AEST 18 September. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Nursing and Midwifery"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146083159": {
      "title": "Research Support Officer",
      "description": "We are seeking a highly organised and motivated Research Support Officer to join the Griffith Business School Research Operations Team. Reporting to the Research Support Manager within the Dean (Research) Office, this position provides professional administrative and operational support for research activities across Griffith Business School. As a shared resource, the Research Support Officer may support activities at Group, Department or Research Entity level. Working under broad direction, the Research Support Officer is responsible for a varied portfolio of research operations, communications, events, projects, systems and administrative processes. The role supports the effective delivery and promotion of the School’s research activities, profile and impact. It is a professional staff position focused on enabling research operations rather than undertaking academic research. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinate operational, administrative and project activities for the Research Operations Team, researchers, committees and working groups, including preparing meeting papers and minutes, tracking action items and supporting agreed outcomes. Develop communications that promote GBS research and impact to internal and external audiences, including LinkedIn articles, research stories, web and intranet content, and other promotional material. Plan and coordinate research events, workshops, seminars and stakeholder engagement activities, managing timelines, communications, logistics and follow-up to support effective delivery. Coordinate the administration of small grant schemes and programs, including application rounds, submissions and records, assessment processes, applicant communications and outcome reporting. Coordinate administrative processes relating to projects, finance and HR, including payments, invoicing, small budgets, routine correspondence and record management, in accordance with relevant University requirements. Gather, prepare and analyse research data, including publications and research income, for meetings, dashboards and reporting. Maintain accurate and up-to-date records for the Research Operations Team. Maintain and support research information and profile platforms, including Symplectic Elements and Griffith Experts, to ensure the accuracy and visibility of GBS research information. Contribute to the delivery and promotion of strategic research programs and initiatives across Griffith Business School, its departments and research entities. Build effective working relationships with academic and professional staff, central University areas and external stakeholders, and use sound judgement to resolve routine matters or escalate issues where appropriate. About you Experience developing research communications, including research stories, academic profiles, website content and social media content. Experience coordinating research seminars, workshops, conferences and stakeholder engagement activities. Experience supporting research teams, research centres, institutes or academic departments including coordinating research-related committees or working groups and preparing research reports & briefing papers to support.  In addition you will have: Highly developed skills in coordinating professional administrative, operational or project activities, with the ability to organise competing priorities and deliver accurate work within agreed timeframes. Strong written communication skills, including the ability to prepare clear and engaging content for different audiences and communication channels. Strong interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to build positive working relationships and communicate effectively with people at different levels. Experience gathering, maintaining and presenting information for reporting, meetings or decision-making, with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. A positive, friendly and team-oriented approach, with a genuine willingness to assist others, share responsibilities and contribute to a supportive and collaborative working environment. What we can offer This is a full-time continuing position based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan). From February 2027 (the anticipated relocation date), your primary work location will be Brisbane CBD – the date of the relocation is yet to be confirmed. As a multi-site university, Griffith may also require work across other locations as needed. Salary Range The full-time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 6 range $94,966 - $101,295 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $111,110 - $118,515 per annum. \n\n At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . How to apply Please note : This position was recently advertised (June 2026) and as such, applicants who have previously applied for this position will not be required to submit a further application. Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the \"About you\", above and the  position description ). If applying through the Indeed jobs board, please include your cover letter in your resume document. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details. Applicants must have Australian working rights. Sponsorship is not offered and successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Khyla Truloff, via email: k.truloff@griffith.edu.au. Closing date:  Sunday, 13th September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Pro Vice Chancellor (Business)"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000146082279": {
      "title": "Global Mobility Adviser (Inbound) - Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast ",
      "description": "About the Opportunity The Global Mobility Adviser (Inbound) plays a key role in supporting Study Abroad and Exchange students throughout their mobility journey at Griffith, enabling them to successfully complete a non-award trimester or year-long study program. The role involves engaging with incoming students from pre-arrival through to program completion, while contributing to the promotion, administration and continuous improvement of inbound mobility programs. Key responsibilities Provide guidance and support to inbound Study Abroad and Exchange students considering non-award study opportunities at Griffith through a range of communication channels, working collaboratively with stakeholders to promote programs and increase student participation. Assist with the administration and processing of applications for inbound mobility for exchange and study abroad programs. Represent Griffith Global Mobility at events, information sessions, expos, Open Day and visits from partner institutions, agents and government delegates. Develop and maintain website content and support the creation of promotional materials to enhance student engagement and participation. Maintain accurate mobility data and records, and prepare reports and statistics as required. Keep current with relevant legislation, regulatory requirements, University policies and emerging trends in international education and student mobility. About you We are seeking a highly organised and student-focused professional who is passionate about international education and delivering exceptional service. The successful candidate will demonstrate: Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to confidently engage with students, staff and external stakeholders. Strong presentation and public speaking skills, including experience delivering information sessions, presentations and promotional activities to diverse audiences. Exceptional administrative skills, with a high level of accuracy, attention to detail and the ability to manage complex processes and competing priorities. A customer-focused approach and genuine commitment to supporting students throughout their global learning experiences. Experience developing, maintaining or promoting content across digital and print channels, including websites, presentations, brochures and other marketing materials. Strong digital literacy and the ability to quickly learn and work across a range of systems and platforms. The ability to work collaboratively as part of a team while taking ownership of tasks and delivering outcomes in a fast-paced environment. Applicant must have full unrestricted work rights for Australia and will be subject to a national criminal history check.  What we can offer \nThis is a full time , continuing position and can be based at Griffith University Gold Coast or Brisbane South (Nathan) campuses. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary  \nHEW 5 – Base range salary - $82,415.66 - $92,456.55 +17% superannuation. Full package range - $96,426.31 - $108,174.16.  \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 13th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. Griffith was created to be a different type of university. You will find we are about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you will achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "International Office"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145902297": {
      "title": "Clinic Support Officer",
      "description": "Griffith Health is seeking a motivated and customer-focused Clinic Support Officer to join our clinic team. This role is ideal for someone who thrives in a fast-paced environment and is passionate about delivering high-quality administrative and client support within a healthcare setting. Reporting to the Reception Team Leader, you will play a key role in ensuring the smooth day-to-day operation of the clinic. As the first point of contact, you will provide professional reception and administrative support to patients, students, and staff, while contributing to a positive and efficient clinic experience. Key Responsibilities Provide exceptional front-line customer service via phone, email, and in person Manage patient enquiries and resolve routine matters, escalating when required Coordinate patient bookings and maintain accurate scheduling records Process payments and perform daily financial reconciliation and reporting Maintain and update clinic systems, records, and databases Assist with patient record management, including documentation and results handling Support general administrative functions, reporting, and correspondence Collaborate with clinic staff to support service delivery and operations About You \nYou are an organised and detail-oriented professional with strong communication skills and a commitment to excellent client service. You will bring: An associate diploma qualification or equivalent relevant experience Experience in administrative or reception roles, ideally within a healthcare or clinic environment Strong organisational and time management skills Confidence using digital systems and databases The ability to handle sensitive information with confidentiality and professionalism A proactive approach and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team A strong client focus, excellent presentation, and a demonstrated ability to contribute effectively within a team environment. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 4 range $76,126 - $80,321 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $89,067 - $93,976 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact the Dental Clinic Manager at  dentalclinicmanager@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Thursday, 10 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145656089": {
      "title": "Patient Management Systems Administrator",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a Patient Management Systems Administrator to join our dedicated term. This role is critical in supporting the day-to-day operations of our clinics by managing and enhancing the University's Patient Management System, ensuring smooth functionality and integration for all users. Your key responsibilities for this role will include: Manage the Patient Management System daily, making necessary configuration changes and corrections. Provide first-line technical support for clinic operations, including PCs and peripherals, and coordinate escalation when necessary. Offer expert advice on policies, procedures, and legal requirements related to Patient Management System, while ensuring system security and protecting against unauthorised access. Collaborate with key stakeholders from various departments to integrate the Patient Management System and develop essential reports using Microsoft SQL Server. Coordinate with Digital Services and vendors for system upgrades, patches, and troubleshooting. Support business analysis, change management, and testing of new systems and upgrades. Ensure systems are up-to-date and maintain business continuity plans. About you \nTo be successful in this position, you will hold an undergraduate degree in Information Technology (or equivalent industry experience) or health care industry experience, preferably in a systems administrator role. In addition to the above, you will demonstrate: Recent experience with MS SQL Server databases including T-SQL programming or project management experience. High level general computer skills including Microsoft 365 Suite expertise and Windows skills. Excellent interpersonal and written communication skills including the ability to interact with a variety of clients to provide a high level of professional customer service. Demonstrated ability to analyse and resolve client and system problems, lead system innovation and optimisation, prioritise work, and meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work effectively as part of a team and independently, and a flexible approach to working outside normal business hours to resolve problems or to assist with carrying out maintenance. What we can offer \nThis is a part-time (50% FTE), fixed term (12 months) position based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 6 range $94,966 - $101,295 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $111,110 - $118,515 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information about this role, please contact Amanda McGuckin. Director of Health Clinics: amanda.mcguckin@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Wednesday, 9 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Griffith Health"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145417859": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Level B) in Nutrition and Dietetics",
      "description": "The  School of Allied Health, Sport and Social Work  is known for leadership and excellence in health education, research, and clinical practice, with a commitment to developing work-ready graduates.  With programs spanning Physiotherapy, Social Work, Nutrition and Dietetics, Exercise Science, Occupational Therapy, and Speech Pathology, we bring together a diverse team of academics who are pioneering change in their fields.  As a Lecturer in Nutrition and Dietetics , you will play an important role in developing knowledgeable, capable and compassionate health professionals. Through innovative teaching, authentic assessment and strong industry connections, you will help students translate evidence into practice and prepare them to respond to evolving health and workforce needs. Key aspects of the role include: Delivering contemporary learning experiences informed by professional practice and emerging evidence. Guiding and supporting students throughout their academic and professional development. Coordinating courses and contributing to a cohesive, high quality program experience. Developing partnerships that expand practical learning and graduate employment opportunities. Collaborating with colleagues to strengthen teaching quality, scholarship and the School’s profile. This is a continuing, full time position primarily based at Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Academic Level B range $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $136,979 - $162,667 per annum. \n\n To be successful in this role, you will bring strong discipline knowledge, a passion for student learning and the ability to connect contemporary evidence with professional practice. You will also demonstrate a collaborative approach and a commitment to delivering an inclusive and high quality student experience. You will have: A doctoral or master’s qualification in nutrition and/or dietetics,  the credential of Accredited Practising Dietitian (APD) or eligibility to become accredited as a dietitian in Australia, and substantial professional experience. Demonstrated experience in teaching, training or supporting learning in a university, clinical or professional environment. Contemporary knowledge of nutrition and dietetics practice, with the ability to translate evidence into engaging learning experiences. Strong communication, collaboration and organisational skills, including the capacity to coordinate courses and academic activities. A commitment to reflective practice, educational innovation and the continuous improvement of student outcomes. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Rozanne Kruger, Professor in Nutrition and Dietetics within School of Allied Health, Sport and Social Work at r.kruger@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Monday 14th September at 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Health Sciences and Social Work"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145413044": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Level B) - Clinical Psychology",
      "description": "The School of Applied Psychology is a national leader in accredited psychology training, with award winning educators and a strong focus on real world clinical skills. Our research drives national and international practice, with strengths in health behaviour, child and family interventions, youth development, workplace wellbeing and cognition. We offer extensive industry partnerships and work integrated learning across all levels of study. As a Lecturer in Clinical Psychology , you will deliver high-quality teaching and clinical training within the School of Applied Psychology. You will support an outstanding, student-centred learning experience across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. As a Lecturer you will: Deliver engaging teaching across psychology, counselling, clinical psychology and professional psychology programs. Provide high quality clinical supervision to postgraduate students. Coordinate courses and contribute to associated academic administration. Improve teaching through evaluation, professional development and scholarly practice. Build positive relationships with students, colleagues, industry and community partners. This is a continuing, part-time (0.60FTE), position primarily based at Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Academic Level B range $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $136,979 - $162,667 per annum. \n\n You are a qualified clinical psychologist committed to evidence-based practice, high quality teaching and the development of future psychology professionals. To be successful, you will have: A PhD or doctoral qualification in clinical psychology or a related field. Current AHPRA registration with an area of practice endorsement in clinical psychology. Eligibility to provide supervision under Psychology Board of Australia requirements. Demonstrated ability to deliver student centred teaching and effective clinical supervision. Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with a commitment to improving teaching practice. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Opportunities for internal mobility How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Allison Waters, Head of School - School of Applied Psychology at HoS-appliedpsychology@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 13th September 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Applied Psychology"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145383519": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Pharmacy ",
      "description": "The School of Pharmacy and Medical Sciences is entering an exciting period of growth. With increasing student demand and a strong commitment to delivering an exceptional pharmacy education experience, we are expanding our Pharmacy team and seeking a Lecturer (Level B) who wants to help shape the next chapter of our success. This is an opportunity to join a collegial and forward-looking school that is preparing graduates for a rapidly evolving healthcare landscape. You'll work alongside passionate educators, researchers and clinicians who are committed to innovation, excellence and making a real difference to the health of communities. Whether your passion lies in pharmacy practice, pharmacotherapy, curriculum innovation, student learning or building stronger connections with the profession, this role offers the opportunity to contribute, influence and grow. You will bring your contemporary pharmacy expertise into the classroom, inspire future pharmacists, and help create learning experiences that connect education with real-world practice. As a Lecturer in Pharmacy, you will contribute to teaching across Pharmacy Practice, Pharmacotherapy and related areas, while also supporting curriculum development, professional engagement and student research opportunities. Key responsibilities include: Developing and delivering engaging teaching and assessment in Pharmacotherapy and/or Pharmacy Practice. Creating an outstanding student experience across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Contributing to course coordination, curriculum development and innovative approaches to learning. Supporting Honours and postgraduate pharmacy students through high-quality research experiences. Building industry and community partnerships and contributing to research that influences pharmacy education and practice. This is a part-time (0.8 FTE), continuing position based at Griffith University’s Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Academic Level B range $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $136,979 - $162,667 per annum. \n\n To be successful in this role, you will hold a degree in Pharmacy and have substantial professional experience relevant to the discipline, including at least 5 years working within a clinical pharmacy environment. You will have: Current professional registration as a Pharmacist with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (Ahpra). Demonstrated knowledge and experience in Pharmacotherapy and/or Pharmacy Practice. Experience teaching Pharmacotherapy and/or Pharmacy Practice in a higher education setting will be highly regarded. A commitment to student-centred learning and the ongoing development of pharmacy education. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the Position Description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Jennifer Boddy, Head of School of Pharmacy and Medical Sciences at j.boddy@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Tuesday, 22 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Pharmacy and Medical Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145374499": {
      "title": "Academic Support Officer",
      "description": "We are seeking an Academic Support Officer to join the team at our Nathan campus. Please note that from February 2027 (the anticipated relocation) this position will move to the Brisbane City (CBD). This role provides a high level of administrative support within the Learning and Teaching portfolio, to ensure effective course management for Griffith Business School. The Academic Support Officer is responsible for supporting a broad range of course administration activities and processes including, but not limited to, the management of enrolments, course profile generation and review, academic administration for minor and major course changes, timely provision of course information, and support for Course Convenors. Working within a designated Program cluster, this role works in collaboration with the Program Support Officers, to drive processes and administrative course support to Departments, Program Directors and Advisors, Course Convenors, and academic staff. About you You will be an experienced administrator with: Excellent communication and interpersonal skills A professional demeanour with a customer-focused approach A high-level of attention to detail Experience with data entry, management and reporting Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications Preferred: Experience working with a Curriculum Management System What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position where you will initially be based at Brisbane South (Nathan). From February 2027 (the anticipated relocation) , your primary work location will be Brisbane CBD – the date of the relocation is yet to be determined. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Salary Range \nBase Salary Hew 5 in the range of $82,415 - $92,456 per annum plus 17% Superannuation. The salary including superannuation is in the range $96,426 -$108,174 per annum. \n\n The occupant will possess relevant tertiary qualifications and subsequent work experience or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education and training. \n\n At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . How to apply \nPlease submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the ‘About you’ section and  Position Description . (If applying through Indeed, please include you cover letter as the first page of your resume).  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have Australian working rights for the duration of this contract. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Lisa Boulton, Team Leader (Academic Services) via email lisa.boulton@griffith.edu.au .  Closing date:  Sunday, 6th September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 3 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Griffith Business School  (GBS) delivers outstanding business education and research through engagement with industry, government and the local communities we serve. \n \nGriffith Business School strives to prepare graduates for an ever-changing world where responsible leadership and sustainable business practices are critical. Our strong industry connections and partnerships with many high-profile organisations across the country and throughout the Asia–Pacific region ensures our graduates are equipped to meet the demands of employers, today and in the future.",
      "department": "Pro Vice Chancellor (Business)"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145368319": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Indigenous Strategic Initiatives",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  Reporting to the Senior Portfolio Manager, the Senior Manager, Indigenous Strategic Initiatives will lead the planning, coordination, and delivery of key Indigenous strategic priorities across Griffith University. Working closely with the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Indigenous) and other senior stakeholders, the role will drive major initiatives, provide strategic advice, and support the development of frameworks and strategies that advance Griffith's Indigenous vision and objectives. Key responsibilities include driving the development and implementation of the University’s Indigenous strategic priorities and overseeing the delivery of the University's Indigenous Plan, ensuring alignment with broader strategic priorities across research, education, people, and internationalisation. Key Responsibilities Lead the planning, coordination, and delivery of key Indigenous strategic initiatives on behalf of the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Indigenous), ensuring successful outcomes within agreed timeframes and resources. Drive the development and implementation of Griffith University's Indigenous strategic priorities, including project governance, stakeholder engagement, risk management, and reporting. Lead the development of Griffith University's Indigenous Plan, facilitating consultation and engagement with Indigenous students, staff, communities, and key stakeholders. Foster strategic partnerships across the University and externally to strengthen Griffith's Indigenous identity, presence, and strategic objectives. Oversee major projects, including the GUMURRII South Bank relocation, ensuring effective planning, stakeholder engagement, governance, and transition management. Aligned to the University’s Research and Innovation Plan, coordinate the refresh of the Indigenous Research Unit in partnership with internal stakeholders, Indigenous researchers, and external partners, including the Alliance 2050 network.   Lead the development of Indigenous recruitment, marketing, and communications initiatives in collaboration with key University stakeholders. Represent the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Indigenous) on committees, working groups, and forums, providing strategic advice and escalating key risks and opportunities. Prepare high-level reports, briefing papers, business cases, and recommendations to support executive decision-making. Monitor project performance, identify emerging risks and opportunities, and provide strategic advice to support the achievement of Indigenous priorities. Build and maintain productive relationships with senior leaders, academic and professional staff, external partners, and First Peoples communities to drive successful project outcomes. About you Completion of a postgraduate degree in a relevant field together with extensive relevant experience; or extensive experience and management expertise; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Demonstrated strong understanding of contemporary Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples, cultures and communities, including knowledge of the higher education sector and the ability to communicate and engage effectively and respectfully with Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples. \nCandidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and may be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer \nThis is a full time, fixed term opportunity for 12 months primarily based at the Logan Campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 10 – Base range - $147,057.74 + 17% superannuation. Full package - $172,057.55. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description.  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Tuesday 8th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online.  \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "PVC Indigenous"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145181380": {
      "title": "Information and Privacy Officer",
      "description": "About the Opportunity  The Information & Privacy Officer is responsible for managing all right to information applications, administrative access applications and privacy complaints received by the University. Key Responsibilities Manage end-to-end processing of RTI, administrative access, and privacy complaints. Provide expert advice and support to staff on RTI, administrative access and privacy obligations. Conduct investigations and recommend findings to the Privacy Officer. Support the management and remediation of Personal Identifying Information Develop and maintain RTI, administrative access and privacy complaint resources for stakeholders. Deliver staff training and education on RTI, administrative access and privacy matters. Maintain accurate records to support compliance and reporting requirements. Monitor legislative changes and update related policies and procedures. About you The occupant of this position will hold relevant undergraduate qualifications (preferably legal qualifications) with at least two (2) years-experience in a similar role or have an equivalent level of expertise obtained from a combination of experience, training, professional accreditation and qualification. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and may be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer \nThis is a full time, continuing opportunity primarily based at Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 7 – Base range - $104,504.60 - $113,219.43 + 17% superannuation. Full package range - $122,270.38 - $132,466.72. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acqusition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 6th September at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "General Counsel"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145045459": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship: Settlement Agreements between First Peoples and Australian governments",
      "description": "We are currently looking for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander PhD candidate to join our Australian Research Council (ARC) Discovery Project on Settlement Agreements between First Peoples and State governments in Victoria and Western Australia. The primary focus of this role is to develop a distinct PhD topic within the Discovery Project parameters that contributes new and innovative knowledge to the field of agreement-making between First Peoples and Australian Governments. The Project investigates how Settlement Agreements are negotiated and implemented and explores the various social, political and cultural dimensions that shape how they are made and managed.  Success in this role requires engagement with First Peoples groups and governments in Victoria or/and Western Australia, and collaboration with a small research team based at Griffith University and James Cook University. \n\n The selection of the applicant for the award of a Higher Degree Research Scholarship at Griffith University is based on academic merit and research background. Expressions of interest are welcome from Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander descent applicants, who identify themselves as such, and are accepted by the community with which they are associated with as an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander. a Bachelor degree with second-class honours (Division A) or a Masters degree, incorporating a significant research component, from a recognised institution; or a record of research, or a qualification granted by a professional or other body deemed by the Dean, Griffith Graduate Research School, to be of a standard comparable to a Bachelor degree with second-class honours (Division A).  Refer to the  Equivalent Qualifications for HDR Program Admission  web page. \n\n About the scholarship \nThe 2026 Griffith University Indigenous Australian Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $55,379 (indexed) for a period of up to four years of full-time study. Please see the  GUIAPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  ciaran.ofaircheallaigh@griffith.edu.au  or  bartholomew.stanford@jcu.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is: Friday 18 September 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nEmeritus Professor Ciaran O’Faircheallaigh \nPhone:  0419-023-741 \nEmail:   ciaran.ofaircheallaigh@griffith.edu.au Dr Bartholomew Stanford \nPhone: 0450-141-374 \nEmail:  bartholomew.stanford@jcu.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The School of Government and International Relations is among the highest-ranked politics schools or departments in Australia. It has received the top ranking of 5, ‘Well above world standard,’ in Political Science/International Relations in the past three Excellence in Research for Australia exercises, dating to 2012. The School consistently ranks among the very top Australian departments for research productivity and external funding, and it ranked first in Queensland in the most recent Shanghai rankings. Our research expertise is leveraged in extensive engagement activities with government and civil society, and in dynamic learning and teaching that has garnered numerous university and external awards The project is led by Emeritus Professor Ciaran O'Faircheallaigh and Dr Bartholomew Stanford. Ciaran has worked with First Peoples in Australia and internationally for over thirty years, and has written extensively about commercial agreements First Peoples have negotiated with mining, oil and gas companies. Bartholomew is a Torres Strait Islander political science researcher who studies Indigenous-state relations. This opportunity will be primarily based at Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144659189": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "At Griffith’s School of Nursing and Midwifery , research drives real-world change. We challenge the status quo, partner with communities and industry, and translate evidence into practice to improve health outcomes for all. Our work spans innovation, policy and care delivery with a strong focus on equity and impact. We support emerging researchers, champion inclusive leadership, and collaborate globally to amplify our reach and results. We are seeking a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow to join a dynamic multidisciplinary research program focused on improving prevention, acute care and recovery outcomes for children experiencing burns and trauma. The successful candidate will contribute to high-quality research spanning clinical studies, implementation science and health services research, working closely with clinicians, consumers, researchers and policy stakeholders to improve outcomes for children and families. Depending on experience, appointments may be made at either the Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow level. As a Research Fellow, you will: Conduct and coordinate research activities that contribute to externally funded research programs focused on paediatric injury and burn care Design and undertake clinical, implementation and health services research projects, including clinical trials and cohort studies Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with clinicians, researchers, consumers and policy stakeholders Contribute to competitive research funding applications and develop a strong publication record Lead the preparation of publications, presentations, policy briefs and research reports Support co-design, stakeholder engagement, knowledge translation and implementation activities Manage research projects to achieve outcomes within agreed timelines, budgets and reporting requirements Assist with the supervision and mentoring of higher degree research candidates, research assistants and project staff. As a Senior Research Fellow, you will: Lead components of externally funded research programs in paediatric injury and burn care Design and oversee clinical trials, cohort studies and implementation research projects Drive interdisciplinary collaborations with clinicians, health services and policy partners Develop and lead competitive research grant applications, including NHMRC and MRFF funding schemes Contribute to strategic research development within the School of Nursing and Midwifery and Griffith Health Lead the production of high-impact publications, reports, policy briefs and conference presentations Drive stakeholder engagement, co-design and knowledge translation initiatives to maximise research impact Provide supervision and mentoring to higher degree research candidates, postdoctoral fellows and research staff Manage multi-site research projects and provide leadership within collaborative research teams. This is a fixed term (two year), full-time position primarily based in Brisbane at the Queensland Children's Hospital. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University's sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range Research Fellow \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be $91,705 - $139,031 per annum plus 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $107,295 - $162,667 per annum. Senior Research Fellow \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be $143,418 - $165,374 per annum plus 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $167,799 - $193,487 per annum. \n\n To be successful in this role, you will bring a passion for improving child health outcomes through collaborative, high-quality research and a commitment to translating evidence into practice. You will also have: A PhD in nursing, public health, psychology, epidemiology, emergency medicine, or another relevant health discipline Demonstrated experience undertaking health or clinical research in a relevant field Strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to work effectively with clinicians, researchers, consumers and community partners Experience contributing to research publications and competitive funding applications Excellent communication, project management and organisational skills. In addition, Senior Research Fellow candidates will demonstrate: Significant postdoctoral research experience and a record of research leadership A strong track record of securing or contributing to competitive research funding Experience leading complex research projects and multidisciplinary collaborations Proven ability to supervise, mentor and develop researchers across career stages A sustained record of high-quality publications and research impact. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Opportunities for internal mobility How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Bronwyn Griffin Deputy Head of School (Research & HDR) at bronwyn.griffin@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au             Closing date:  Sunday 13th September at 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Nursing and Midwifery"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144651369": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Glycoproteomics in Cytokine Structure Biology",
      "description": "The Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at Griffith University embodies multidisciplinary expertise in glycoscience, biomedicine, structural biology and translational research. Our researchers use advanced technologies and collaborative approaches to understand the molecular basis of health and disease and translate fundamental discoveries into better health outcomes. We are seeking a highly motivated Research Fellow to join an exciting interdisciplinary project investigating how protein glycosylation regulates cytokine signalling during erythropoiesis and how this knowledge can be applied to improve the ex vivo production of red blood cells. The position is supported by the National Health and Medical Research Council Ideas Grant: Manufacturing Red Blood Cells: The Critical Role of Glycosylation in Cytokine Signalling. This $1.67 million collaborative project between Griffith University and Australian Red Cross Lifeblood will investigate how the glycosylation of key cytokines and their receptors influences erythroid-cell proliferation, differentiation, maturation and function. Red blood cell production is controlled by a complex network of cytokines and cell-surface receptors. Although many of these signalling proteins are extensively glycosylated, the ways in which their carbohydrate structures affect protein recognition, receptor activation and cellular outcomes remain poorly understood. The project will address this important knowledge gap and apply the resulting insights to the development of more efficient approaches for producing red blood cells outside the body. Improving ex vivo red blood cell production could contribute to future safe and reliable blood supplies, particularly for patients with rare blood types or complex transfusion requirements. The project brings together an interdisciplinary team with expertise in: Glycomics, proteomics and glycoproteomics Protein biochemistry and structural biology Cytokine and receptor signalling Ex vivo human erythropoiesis Red blood cell physiology and blood rheology The successful candidate will work with the research groups of Professor Daniel Kolarich, Dr Larissa Dirr and Professor Michael Simmonds at Griffith University, together with Dr Becky Griffiths at Australian Red Cross Lifeblood. This appointment offers an outstanding opportunity to develop an interdisciplinary research profile at the interface of glycoscience, structural biology, cell biology and transfusion research. Key responsibilities As the Research Fellow, you will: Design, conduct and analyse research investigating the role of protein glycosylation in cytokine signalling and erythroid development. Investigate selected cytokines and their receptors using complementary biochemical, glycomic, glycoproteomic, structural and cell-biological approaches. Contribute to experiments using ex vivo erythroid culture systems to determine how changes in glycosylation affect cell proliferation, differentiation, maturation and function. Work with project specialists to relate molecular and cellular changes to the functional, biophysical and rheological properties of developing and mature red blood cells. Develop, optimise and validate experimental protocols relevant to the project. Integrate and interpret results produced through multiple experimental platforms and participating laboratories. Apply rigorous approaches to experimental design, quantitative analysis, research reproducibility and data management. Prepare manuscripts for publication in high-quality peer-reviewed journals. Present research findings at project meetings and national and international scientific conferences. Coordinate research activities with investigators, research staff, students and external collaborators. Contribute to the supervision and development of honours and higher degree research students, where appropriate. Assist with project reporting, research governance, ethics, biosafety and laboratory compliance requirements. Contribute to the development of new grant applications and collaborative opportunities arising from the research. Participate actively in the research cultures of Griffith University, the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics and Australian Red Cross Lifeblood. About you \nYou will hold a PhD or equivalent qualification in biochemistry, molecular biology, glycobiology, proteomics, structural biology, cell biology, haematology or another discipline relevant to the project. You will have strong research experience in at least one of the project’s core areas and a genuine interest in learning and applying complementary approaches from the other participating disciplines. Applicants are not expected to already be experts in every area of the project. Specialist training and mentorship will be available through the interdisciplinary research team. To be successful in this position, you will demonstrate: Advanced research expertise in one or more of the following areas: Glycomics, glycoproteomics or mass-spectrometry-based proteomics Protein biochemistry, recombinant protein production or protein–protein interaction analysis Structural or biophysical characterisation of proteins Cytokine biology or cell-signalling research Mammalian or primary human cell culture Haematopoietic stem and progenitor cell biology or ex vivo erythropoiesis Red blood cell physiology, biomechanics or blood rheology The ability to design, conduct, troubleshoot and interpret complex experiments. A sound understanding of quantitative analysis, appropriate experimental controls and research reproducibility. A record of high-quality research outputs appropriate to career stage, including peer-reviewed publications. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative and multidisciplinary research program. A willingness and capacity to acquire new experimental and analytical skills across the project’s research areas. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate findings to researchers from different disciplinary backgrounds. Strong organisational and project-management skills, including the ability to manage competing priorities and deliver agreed milestones. A commitment to research integrity, laboratory safety, collegiality and an inclusive research culture. Experience in interdisciplinary research, collaborative project delivery or the supervision of research students would be advantageous. What we can offer \nThis is a three-year, fixed-term, full-time position primarily based within the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at Griffith University’s Gold Coast campus. The successful candidate will be required to undertake research activities at other Griffith University or Australian Red Cross Lifeblood locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe successful candidate will be appointed at either Research Fellow Grade 1 or Research Fellow Grade 2 , depending on their qualifications, skills, experience and demonstrated level of research expertise. Base salary range for Research Fellow Grade 1 $91,705 - $107,420 per annum + 17% superannuation.  \nBase salary range for Research Fellow Grade 2 $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% supernnauation. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:   Access to advanced glycomics, glycoproteomics, mass spectrometry, structural biology, cell-culture and blood-rheology capabilities. Mentorship from investigators with complementary expertise across molecular glycoscience, structural biology, erythropoiesis and red blood cell physiology. Close collaboration with Australian Red Cross Lifeblood and exposure to a nationally significant translational research environment. Opportunities to establish national and international collaborations. Support for high-quality publications, conference participation and professional development. Opportunities to contribute to research student supervision, grant development and future translational research. Griffith University employment benefits, including 17% employer superannuation, generous leave provisions, salary packaging and professional development opportunities. How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Visa sponsorship may be available to the successful candidate. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Daniel Kolarich, Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at  d.kolarich@griffith.edu.au    Closing date:  11:59pm AEST 20 September 2026.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144457779": {
      "title": "Global Safety Innovation PhD Scholarship: Improving the measurement and reporting of safety information within organisations",
      "description": "This PhD is grounded in real world practice. Global Safety Innovation (GSI) is a network of organisations across high-risk industries working toward safer workplaces. Rather than researching safety measurement from the outside, you will work directly with GSI's partner organisations throughout the PhD, spending focused blocks of time embedded within their operations to see firsthand how safety data and reporting systems are built, used, and contested. Success will mean working across two worlds: rigorous methodology with academic supervisors, and genuine collaboration with practitioners inside partner organisations. The project aims to improve understanding of the conditions under which innovation in safety measurement is possible, exploring questions such as: What factors increase or decrease the social acceptability of alternative measures within and beyond organisations? What makes a new measure practically useful for improving safety? And how is consensus around new measures established, maintained, and translated into practice? Your supervision team for this project brings together expertise in organisational safety, community-engaged research, and human factors. You will be supervised by Dr Clarissa Carden, an interdisciplinary researcher specialising in community safety, wellbeing, and social justice; Associate Professor Drew Rae, a leading safety researcher whose work examines how organisations manage risk and safety performance; and Zoë Nation, a human factors and safety specialist focused on improving the systems, practices, and behaviours that shape safety outcomes. This position is primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful within this role, a background in sociology or safety science is desirable. You will have evidence of a passion for social research, with a strong desire to undertake practical, applied scholarship to respond to industry challenges. Ideally you will have had some experience in heavy industry or applying safety in a working context. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to  second  class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the  Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . International applicants to research programs must demonstrate they meet the program’s English Language Proficiency requirements, as outlined below: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR an overall score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 (applies to tests taken prior to 21 January 2026) OR an overall score of 4 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with a minimum score of 4 in each of the listening, reading, and writing skills, and a minimum score of 3.5 in speaking skills (applies to tests taken on or after 21 January 2026) OR a score of no less than 3+ in each skill of the ISLPR (conducted by ISLPR Language Services only) OR a minimum overall score of 176 (no score less than 169) on C1 Advanced  or  C2 Proficiency OR an overall score of 58 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 50 (applies to tests taken prior to 7 August 2025) OR an overall score of 55 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 47 (applies to tests taken on or after 7 August 2025) OR an overall score of 70 in the LanguageCert Academic OR an overall score of 59 in the Michigan English Test (MET). English test results must have been obtained within two years of the program commencement date, and only in-person test results will be accepted. \n\n About the scholarship \nThis scholarship will be jointly funded by Global Safety Innovation and Griffith University. The total scholarship value is $50,000 per annum for three years, with the possibility of a six-month extension. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study at a university that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in Southeast Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility, and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to ' clarissa.carden@griffith.edu.au ' , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Friday 11th of September 2026, at 11:59PM AEST. The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nDr Clarissa Carden \nEmail:  clarissa.carden@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact:  \nGriffith Graduate Research School  \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The School of Humanities, Languages and Social Science proudly hosts world-class researchers in a diverse range of specialist fields. We are home to the Griffith Centre for Social and Cultural Research (GCSCR), which is dedicated to increasing our understanding of current challenges facing the modern world. Through the application of innovative humanities and social science research, incorporating the knowledge of academics from a broad range of interrelated disciplines, we are at the cutting edge of humanities and social science discourse. We are also home to the Safety Science Innovation Lab which was founded to capitalise on the opportunities for collaboration between science, health, business and the humanities and social sciences. Our approach recognises that critical innovations in safety science won't necessarily come from new technologies, but from a better understanding of complexity and social construction. The successful applicant will be based at either Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or the Gold Coast.  Remote study may be available, subject to approval. The project will require regular collaboration with Global Safety Innovation Summit (GSI), including focused blocks of time throughout the PhD embedded within GSI or participating companies. The supervisors for this project will be Dr Clarissa Carden, Associate Professor Drew Rae, and Zoe Nation, Director of Global Safety Innovation.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144456009": {
      "title": "Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services",
      "description": "Join our supportive and dedicated Researcher Services team!  As a Discipline Librarian reporting to the Coordinator Researcher Services, you will:  Foster collaborative relationships across the Academic Engagement Services team, the wider Library portfolio, and with key university entities including the Office for Research, eResearch, and Griffith Graduate Research School. Work closely with your colleagues and with other teams across the Library to provide advice to researchers and HDR candidates on topics such as working with literature, scholarly publishing, researcher profiles and metrics and working with data.   Develop and evaluate effectiveness of researcher training workshops and eLearning resources.  Provide discipline-relevant scholarly information via consultation services with researchers and HDR candidates.   The role involves interacting face-to-face and virtually with academic staff and students. As such, you will be innovative, open-minded, approachable, collaborative, and have excellent communication, leadership and teamwork skills This is an exciting opportunity to develop your skills within a strong ‘One Library’ culture.    About you   Qualifications in librarianship, information management or equivalent. Experience in an academic or research library, and an understanding of the evolving research environment within universities. Demonstrated experience in developing and delivering workshops and delivering online consultations is desirable. Demonstrated knowledge of supporting systematic-style literature reviews, scholarly publishing, researcher profiles and metrics. Experience with use of research technologies and research literature focused AI tools. Good communication skills with strong client support, teaching, eLearning resource creation, and problem-solving skills.  You will be comfortable operating in a rapidly changing environment. Being flexible, open-minded, approachable, collaborative, well-organised and having a strong teamwork ethic is vital. You will be committed to a focus on continuous improvement.   What we can offer     Two continuing full-time Discipline Librarian roles:   Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services (Business)  Discipline Librarian, Researcher Services (Science)  The full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 7: $104,504 – $113,219 p.a. + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be $122,270 – $132,466 per annum. Your primary campus location for: Researcher Services (Business) will be based at Brisbane South (Nathan) Researcher Services (Science) at Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations.   Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.  \n\n   \n\n At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information, please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy . Why Griffith?  As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We support the professional and personal development of all our employees and invest in the skills of our people. We offer:  mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment.  salary packaging options and corporate health discounts.  generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading.  support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme. How to apply  Please provide the following:  A cover letter   Addressing your suitability for the position (not more than 2 pages)  Refer to the 'About you' section above and the Position Description   Indicate your preference for the Business or Sciences Discipline Librarian and primary campus location - Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast.   Your current CV/Resume  Applicants must have Australian working rights for the duration of this contract. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. Sponsorship is not offered for this position.  For further information specifically about this role , please contact Ryan Weymouth, Coordinator, Researcher Services at r.weymouth@griffith.edu.au or via Teams.  For application or recruitment support please contact Kate Watson - Talent Acquisition Partner via email: kate.watson@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Wednesday 2nd September 2026 AEST at 11:59 pm. All applications must be submitted online.   \n\n This is an exciting opportunity to join Griffith Library's Academic Engagement Services team.  You will help Griffith researchers and Higher Degree Research (HDR) candidates to excel through the provision of integrated, high-impact researcher support services.  We are seeking two dynamic and proactive Discipline Librarians to join our Researcher Services team to support researchers and HDR candidates respectively from Griffith’s Business School and Griffith’s Sciences Group.",
      "department": "Library"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144455125": {
      "title": "PhD scholarship: Mode of Action Studies of Marine Bacillus Probiotics Using Multi-omics Approaches",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to undertake research on the mode of action of marine  Bacillus  probiotics using advanced multi-omics approaches. This project forms part of a larger collaborative research initiative between Griffith University, Bioproton Pty Ltd and the Marine Bioproducts Cooperative Research Centre (MBCRC). The project provides an opportunity to develop high-level skills in microbiology, probiotic research, metabolomics, microbiome analysis, bioinformatics and systems biology, while working in a collaborative academic–industry environment. The successful candidate will engage with industry partners and MBCRC to gain insight into product development, regulatory requirements and commercial translation, and will be supported to disseminate research outcomes through publications, presentations and collaborative activities relevant to Australia’s growing marine bioproducts sector. This position is primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. \n\n The successful applicant should have a strong academic background in microbiology, biotechnology, biochemistry, molecular biology, bioinformatics, or a related discipline. You will have evidence of curiosity, initiative and a strong interest in applying advanced research methods to understand host–microbe interactions and the mechanisms underpinning probiotic efficacy. The successful applicant will be expected to work both independently and as part of a collaborative research team, communicate effectively with academic and industry partners, and contribute to publications, presentations and project reporting. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants and international applicants. If you are an international applicant who is currently offshore please consider the viability of travelling costs before submitting an expression of interest. Please see the  FAQs  web page for requirements for offshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with First Class Honours (H1) or equivalent, or a master's degree (AQF Level 9) with a substantial research component demonstrating academic excellence. Honours Class 2A or equivalent are encouraged to apply. Masters degree incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the  Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . Applicants are advised that the following skills are highly desirable: Strong analytical and problem-solving, with the ability to design and conduct independent research. Experience with microbiome analysis, next-generation sequencing, bioinformatics, molecular microbiology, cell culture, immunological assays, metabolomics or systems biology approaches. Experience working in industry, clinical, translational or collaborative research environments. Evidence of competitive scholarships, academic awards or research excellence prizes. Programming and data analysis skills \n\n About the scholarship \nThis scholarship is a MBCRC PhD scholarship providing a stipend of AUD $60,000 per annum for a period of up to 3.5 years, subject to satisfactory progress and continued enrolment in the doctoral program. The scholarship is intended to support a full-time PhD candidate undertaking this industry-linked research project. The scholarship will be awarded in accordance with Griffith University’s higher degree research scholarship conditions and any applicable project funding requirements. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with Griffith University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). At the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics, you will join a collaborative and multidisciplinary research environment with access to experienced supervisors, advanced laboratory facilities and expertise across microbiology, natural product chemistry, bioanalysis, glycomics, metabolomics and translational biomedical research. The Institute provides a supportive setting for HDR candidates to develop as independent researchers while engaging with interdisciplinary teams and external partners. Through this project, you will be supported to build technical and analytical capability in multi-omics approaches, microbiome and metabolomic data interpretation, probiotic mechanism-of-action studies and industry-relevant research translation. The project will also provide opportunities to interact with Bioproton Pty Ltd and MBCRC, enabling you to gain practical insight into industry-led research priorities, product development pathways, regulatory considerations and commercial translation. You will also have opportunities to contribute to publications, present findings to academic and industry audiences, and gain experience working within a research program connected to Australia’s emerging marine bioproducts sector. EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to Associate Professor Yun Feng at  ' y.feng@griffith.edu.au ' , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Friday 18 September 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nAssociate Professor Yun Feng \nEmail:  y.feng@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n This scholarship will be based within Griffith University’s Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics, a multidisciplinary biomedical research institute that brings together expertise in discovery and translational research, advanced infrastructure and research training. Candidates will join a collaborative and well-equipped research environment that supports innovation from fundamental discovery through to applied and translational outcomes. The successful applicant will be based at Griffith University’s Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. The Principal Supervisor for this project, Associate Professor Yun Feng, is a Research Leader at Griffith University’s Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics whose research focuses on natural products, marine microbiology, and biotechnology. Her work uses bioactive compounds from nature to advance treatments for neurological diseases and develop innovative solutions for animal health, food safety, and agriculture.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000143740949": {
      "title": "Executive Officer (Digital Health and Innovation)",
      "description": "We are seeking an experienced and highly organised Executive Officer to join the Health Group and play a pivotal role in supporting the Director of Digital Health and Innovation to deliver strategic initiatives that shape the future of digital health education, research and workforce capability. This newly created position offers an exciting opportunity to work at the intersection of strategy, innovation and operational excellence within one of Australia's leading health education environments. \n \n About the Opportunity \nReporting to the Director, Digital Health and Innovation, you will play a key enabling role in supporting the coordination and implementation of strategic digital health and innovation initiatives across the Health Group. You will provide high-level strategic, operational and project support, coordinate projects and deliverables, improve systems and processes, prepare high-quality briefings and reports, and support evidence-informed decision-making across a diverse and evolving portfolio. Working collaboratively across academic, professional and clinical teams, you will contribute to strategic planning, project implementation, governance, reporting and continuous improvement activities that advance the University's digital health agenda. About you \nYou are an experienced project and operations professional who enjoys working in a dynamic and collaborative environment. You have exceptional organisational skills, exercise sound judgement, and are confident managing multiple priorities while supporting senior leaders. You will bring: Relevant tertiary qualifications with substantial relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, skills and experience. Demonstrated experience providing high-level executive, strategic or project support within a complex organisation. Strong project coordination skills with the ability to manage competing priorities and deliver outcomes. Excellent analytical, research and problem-solving skills, with the ability to prepare high-quality reports, briefings and recommendations. Experience developing and improving systems, workflows and business processes to support operational efficiency. Outstanding written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build productive relationships across a diverse range of stakeholders. Experience supporting governance, quality assurance, accreditation or continuous improvement activities will be highly regarded. Strong digital literacy and the ability to leverage technology to improve business processes, support evidence-informed decision-making, and enhance project delivery. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 7 range $104,504 - $113,219 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $122,270 - $132,466 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description )  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Gary Grant, Director Digital Health and Innovation) at g.grant@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Monday, 7 September 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Pro Vice Chancellor (Health)"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000143189369": {
      "title": "Industry PhD Scholarship - Solving Plastic Waste CRC: Tracing the fate of biodegradable plastics in soil-plant systems",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to join our Soil Biogeochemistry and Environmental Sustainability Research Group at Griffith University. The project will investigate the environmental fate of biodegradable plastics in soil–plant systems, with a focus on understanding how these materials degrade, whether they generate residual micro- and nano-plastics, and their potential transfer into agricultural food systems. The research will involve laboratory, greenhouse, and analytical studies conducted in collaboration with the Solving Plastic Waste CRC (SPW CRC) and industry partners. Biodegradable plastics are increasingly promoted as sustainable alternatives to conventional plastics in agriculture and packaging. However, their long-term behaviour in soil remains poorly understood. It is unclear whether these materials fully biodegrade or fragment into persistent micro- and nano-plastic particles that may accumulate in soils or be taken up by plants. This project aims to generate new knowledge on the transformation pathways, persistence, and environmental risks associated with biodegradable plastics in terrestrial ecosystems. Key project objectives include: Investigating the degradation and fragmentation pathways of biodegradable plastics under different soil conditions. Quantifying the formation, abundance, and persistence of residual micro- and nano-plastic particles in soil. Assessing plant uptake, translocation, and potential accumulation of degraded plastic particles within plant tissues. Evaluating the implications of biodegradable plastic degradation for soil health, environmental sustainability, and food-chain exposure. The project will leverage Griffith University’s advanced soil and environmental research facilities and benefit from collaboration with the Solving Plastic Waste CRC, RMIT University, and industry stakeholders. The research will provide critical evidence to support sustainable material design, environmental risk assessment, and policy development, helping guide Australia's transition towards a circular economy and more sustainable agricultural systems. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful in this role, you will hold a qualification in environmental science, soil science, chemistry, environmental engineering, biogeochemistry, materials science, or a related discipline. You will have a strong interest in environmental sustainability and a passion for undertaking research on the degradation, environmental fate, and impacts of biodegradable plastics in soil–plant systems. Experience with laboratory research, soil–plant interactions, or environmental analytical techniques will be advantageous. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to  second  class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the  Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . \n\n About the scholarship \nThis PhD scholarship is funded through the Solving Plastic Waste Cooperative Research Centre (SPW CRC) and Griffith University. The scholarship provides a tax-free stipend of AUD $42,000 per annum for up to three years. In addition, the project includes AUD $10,000 per annum in research support funding to cover project-related research expenses. Please note that the scholarship is subject to Griffith University Higher Degree Research scholarship conditions and satisfactory academic progress throughout candidature. Candidates will be expected to participate in collaborative research activities and contribute to publications, presentations, and thesis outputs arising from the project. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study at a university that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in Southeast Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility, and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to ' m.rezaeirashti@griffith.edu.au ' , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date of expressions of interest:  Friday the 11th of September 2026 at 5pm AEST. The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nDr Mehran Rezaei Rashti  \nEmail:  m.rezaeirashti@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The Soil Biogeochemistry and Environmental Sustainability Research Group at Griffith University conducts internationally recognised research focused on soil health, sustainable agriculture, environmental resilience, and the environmental fate of emerging contaminants. Our interdisciplinary team works across soil science, environmental chemistry, and ecosystem sustainability to deliver innovative, solution-driven research that supports a circular economy and a sustainable future. We collaborate closely with industry, government, and research partners to translate scientific discoveries into practical environmental and agricultural outcomes. This position is primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan), within the School of Environment and Science and the Australian Rivers Institute. As Griffith is a multi-campus University, you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000142743249": {
      "title": "Salesforce Opportunities",
      "description": "Griffith University is making a significant, multi-year investment in our Salesforce ecosystem as part of a broader digital transformation strategy. Backed by executive sponsorship and long-term funding, this enterprise-wide program is reshaping how the university engages with students, alumni, staff and the wider community through modern, connected digital experiences. As Griffith continues its transition to a product-led operating model, we are seeking multiple Salesforce professionals to play key roles in this high-profile transformation. Opportunities are available across product management, platform leadership, business analysis, solution architecture, delivery, administration and Salesforce functional specialisations. Why Join? This is far more than a technology implementation. It is a strategic transformation program that will redefine customer engagement, student lifecycle management, advancement and marketing capabilities across the institution. Successful candidates will have the opportunity to: Contribute to a large-scale, enterprise Salesforce transformation. Work within a highly visible program supported by senior executive leadership. Influence strategic decisions that shape the future digital experience of thousands of users. Partner with a diverse range of stakeholders across academic, professional and technology teams. Help establish and mature a product-led culture focused on value, innovation and continuous improvement. Join an organisation committed to investing in modern technologies, people and ways of working. Who We're Looking For We are interested in professionals who bring a combination of Salesforce expertise, strategic thinking and stakeholder engagement skills. Whether your background is in product leadership, platform management, architecture, business transformation or delivery, you'll be passionate about leveraging Salesforce to drive meaningful outcomes and improve customer experiences at scale. Be Part of Something Bigger If you're looking to make a lasting impact through a complex and highly strategic Salesforce transformation program, we'd love to hear from you. This is a unique opportunity to join a forward-thinking organisation and help shape the future of digital engagement in one of Queensland's leading institutions. To explore these opportunities and find out more please reach out to Jason Pretorius, Cox Purtell on  jasonp@coxpurtell.com.au or 049 9141 822",
      "department": "Digital Solutions"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000142549260": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Manager (Medtech)",
      "description": "As the Manager, Commercialisation (Medtech) you will be responsible for identifying, developing and leading commercialisation opportunities, partnerships, transactions and strategic initiatives in the Medtech area as part of the Life Science team focussing on commercialisation of technologies such as medical devices and digital health. You will work closely with Griffith researchers to identify and assess potential new intellectual property and through business development activities negotiate commercialisation transactions such as licences, start-ups, research agreements to take the research to the market. This will include researchers across all of Griffith’s campuses including the Australian Centre for Precision Health and Technology at Griffith. You will play a key role in advancing commercialisation awareness and skills across the University and, along with key Griffith staff, engaging with partners to help shape strategic partnerships.  As a senior member of the team, you will lead all aspects of the commercialisation and IP management process, from early-stage research to the negotiations of deals and establishing start-up companies. About you \nTo be successful in this role you will hold a postgraduate degree and 5+ years’ experience in a similar role and a strong track record of success in identifying, managing and commercialising intellectual property in the field of Medtech based research through licences, research collaborations and/or start-ups. You will also have technical knowledge in a relevant area of Medtech/digital health and demonstrated business development expertise and a track record will be required. Your ability to deal with complex situations and communicate clearly will be an asset to the team. Demonstrated skills required to assess early-stage technologies and understand intellectual property (IP) management and protection, including patents and other forms of IP; identify and negotiate commercial opportunities; and a proven ability to scope, pitch and negotiate commercialisation transactions are essential. You will need to be a self-starter and team player. You will be given the autonomy, responsibility and required support to make a significant contribution to Griffith Enterprise and the University. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position and will be primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations.  Griffith provides access to fleet cars if needed to travel between its locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nA competitive remuneration package will be negotiated with the successful applicant. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About you section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details A non-confidential deal sheet highlighting your relevant deal track record Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact griffithenterprise@griffth.edu.au . For further application and recruitment advice, please contact Theresa Burke, Talent Acquisition Partner on t.burke@griffith.edu.au  quoting reference number REF11027P . Closing date:   Sunday, 6 September 2026   at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Exciting opportunity to join a dynamic commercialisation team to lead and drive commercialisation and commercial partnerships for world leading research in the Medtech area. Playing a pivotal role in supporting Griffith's ambitions is Griffith Enterprise , the university’s dedicated commercialisation office, managing and commercialising the universities intellectual property and research expertise. Griffith Enterprise’s mission is to manage and facilitate the commercialisation of intellectual property and research excellence from Griffith University researchers via the most appropriate commercialisation pathway, ensuring optimum outcomes for all of our partners whilst protecting and enhancing the reputation of the university. By translating and commercialising ground-breaking technologies and research excellence, empowering a culture of collaboration, and prioritising our partner’s needs we help them achieve their strategic ambitions and generate global impact.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000142550379": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Manager (Medtech)",
      "description": "As the Manager, Commercialisation (Medtech) you will be responsible for identifying, developing and leading commercialisation opportunities, partnerships, transactions and strategic initiatives in the Medtech area as part of the Life Science team focussing on commercialisation of technologies such as medical devices and digital health. You will work closely with Griffith researchers to identify and assess potential new intellectual property and through business development activities negotiate commercialisation transactions such as licences, start-ups, research agreements to take the research to the market. This will include researchers across all of Griffith’s campuses including the Australian Centre for Precision Health and Technology at Griffith. You will play a key role in advancing commercialisation awareness and skills across the University and, along with key Griffith staff, engaging with partners to help shape strategic partnerships.  As a senior member of the team, you will lead all aspects of the commercialisation and IP management process, from early-stage research to the negotiations of deals and establishing start-up companies. About you \nTo be successful in this role you will hold a postgraduate degree and 5+ years’ experience in a similar role and a strong track record of success in identifying, managing and commercialising intellectual property in the field of Medtech based research through licences, research collaborations and/or start-ups. You will also have technical knowledge in a relevant area of Medtech/digital health and demonstrated business development expertise and a track record will be required. Your ability to deal with complex situations and communicate clearly will be an asset to the team. Demonstrated skills required to assess early-stage technologies and understand intellectual property (IP) management and protection, including patents and other forms of IP; identify and negotiate commercial opportunities; and a proven ability to scope, pitch and negotiate commercialisation transactions are essential. You will need to be a self-starter and team player. You will be given the autonomy, responsibility and required support to make a significant contribution to Griffith Enterprise and the University. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position and will be primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations.  Griffith provides access to fleet cars if needed to travel between its locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nA competitive remuneration package will be negotiated with the successful applicant. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About you section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details A non-confidential deal sheet highlighting your relevant deal track record Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact griffithenterprise@griffth.edu.au . For further application and recruitment advice, please contact Theresa Burke, Talent Acquisition Partner on t.burke@griffith.edu.au  quoting reference number REF11027P . Closing date:   Sunday, 6 September 2026   at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Exciting opportunity to join a dynamic commercialisation team to lead and drive commercialisation and commercial partnerships for world leading research in the Medtech area. Playing a pivotal role in supporting Griffith's ambitions is Griffith Enterprise , the university’s dedicated commercialisation office, managing and commercialising the universities intellectual property and research expertise. Griffith Enterprise’s mission is to manage and facilitate the commercialisation of intellectual property and research excellence from Griffith University researchers via the most appropriate commercialisation pathway, ensuring optimum outcomes for all of our partners whilst protecting and enhancing the reputation of the university. By translating and commercialising ground-breaking technologies and research excellence, empowering a culture of collaboration, and prioritising our partner’s needs we help them achieve their strategic ambitions and generate global impact.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000142043849": {
      "title": "CSIRO Industry PhD Scholarship - Training Humanoid Robots for High-Risk Applications using Large and Small AI Models",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to develop trustworthy artificial intelligence for humanoid robots operating in hazardous environments. The primary focus of this role is to integrate computer vision, large and small AI models, formal reasoning, reinforcement learning, and robot control so that humanoid robots can interpret mission goals, plan safe actions, and perform complex manipulation tasks. This CSIRO Industry PhD project includes hands-on research using robotic platforms, multimodal sensors, simulation environments, and industry-relevant scenarios in explosive ordnance disposal, mining, and construction. Success in this role requires close collaboration with researchers and engineers from Griffith University, CSIRO, and Cyborg Dynamics Engineering. This position is based primarily at Griffith University’s Brisbane South (Nathan) campus for three days per week, with the remaining two days spent at our industry partner, Cyborg Dynamics. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. \n\n The selection of applicants for higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University considers academic merit and research experience. To succeed in this role, you will have a strong background in artificial intelligence, computer science, robotics, machine learning, or a related field, together with proficiency in Python and modern machine learning frameworks. You will demonstrate a passion for robotics and trustworthy AI, strong problem-solving and communication skills, and a desire to undertake industry-engaged research in computer vision, reinforcement learning, embodied intelligence, and autonomous systems. Expressions of interest are welcome from Australian or New Zealand citizens, or Australian permanent residents ONLY. Applicants must have completed a Bachelor’s degree with honours equivalent to  first-class honours , or a Master’s degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a Bachelor honours degree, or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Policy . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program, refer  here . Applicants should have an academic background in robotics and AI. \n\n About the scholarship \nThis scholarship offers a generous stipend of $48,071 per annum (indexed) for four years, based on the 2026 rate, plus additional funding for project expenses and professional development starting from $13,000 per year (indexed). What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  z.hou@griffith.edu.au , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Brief research proposal, maximum 2 pages Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Friday 25 September 2026 at 5pm AEST. The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nDr Zhe Hou \nEmail:  z.hou@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nThe PhD Partnerships Team \nEmail:  PhDPartnerships@griffith.edu.au \n\n Griffith University’s School of Information and Communication Technology delivers industry-focused teaching and research across artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, data science, software engineering, robotics and digital technologies. Its researchers develop intelligent systems that address real-world challenges, supported by strong partnerships with industry, government and the wider community. The supervisors for this PhD Project will be Dr Zhe Hou and Dr Ernest Foo.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000142017299": {
      "title": "Lecturer Level B (Speech Pathology)",
      "description": "We are seeking a Lecturer (Level B) in Speech Pathology to join our team in a role that contributes to our teaching, research and professional practice education. You will play a key role in designing and delivering high-quality student learning opportunities within the Masters of Speech Pathology program while supporting broader program learning and teaching foci such as simulation, interprofessional education, and cultural safety. Ideally , y Your expertise will contribute to cutting-edge speech pathology research, ensuring that our programs remain at the forefront of evidence-based practice.   As a Lecturer in Speech Pathology , you will: Develop and deliver engaging, evidence-based teaching for students enrolled in the Masters of Speech Pathology Collaborate with academic staff within the discipline and across disciplines to address speech pathology professional standards and accreditation requirements Conduct and contribute to research in speech pathology related areas Support the discipline to provide culturally safe education for students and prepare them for culturally safe practice . If you are a passionate, forward-thinking speech pathologist looking to make a difference in health care, education, and research , we want to hear from you! This is a full time, continuing position primarily based at Griffith University Gold Coast campus. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Level B range $117,076 - $ 139,031 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $136,979 - $162,667 per annum. Why Join Us?   Work in a collaborative and supportive team environment Opportunities for professional development and research growth Engage with interdisciplinary teams Make a real impact by educating the next generation of speech pathologists The Location & Lifestyle You Deserve   \nThis role is based in stunning South-east Queensland, based at our Gold Coast campus . Enjoy a work-life balance like no other, world-famous beaches, rainforests, and Brisbane, the host city of the 2032 Olympics, all within easy reach.  \n\n To be successful in this role, you will bring a passion for teaching and an innovative approach to student learning. You will have experience in teaching and/or supervising students in clinical and/or university settings across a range of speech pathology practice areas. \n \nIdeally, you will have expertise in child speech, disability and/or augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) to complement the strengths of our existing team; however, this is not essential. \n \nThis role may suit an applicant with a PhD and an emerging research track record who is looking to further strengthen our teaching and research reputation. It may also suit an experienced speech pathologist who has commenced, or is willing to undertake, a PhD and is seeking to transition from professional practice into a university environment while drawing on significant industry experience. \n \nYou will hold membership of, or be eligible for membership with, Speech Pathology Australia. \n\n How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy. Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Sally Hewat, School of Allied Health, Sport and Social Work - Speech Pathology at s.hewat@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 6th September at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Griffith University is a progressive, values-led university dedicated to learning, leadership, and innovation. This is your chance to help us achieve our vision to transform lives and add to human knowledge and understanding in a way that creates a future that benefits all. We are known for our high impact research, outstanding student experience, our commitment to social justice and our welcoming environment. We rank in the top 2% of universities worldwide and we're proud of our remarkable achievements, of our people and their passion for making a positive difference. Together we contribute to the development of knowledge and learning in an attractive environment which values excellence, equity, diversity, and innovation.  The School of Allied Health, Sport and Social Work  is a leader in health education, research, and clinical practice, known for its innovative approach and commitment to developing work-ready graduates.  With programs spanning Physiotherapy, Social Work, Nutrition and Dietetics, Exercise Science, Occupational Therapy, and Speech Pathology , we bring together a diverse team of academics who are pioneering change in their fields.   Since 2012, Speech Pathology at Griffith has played a major role in delivering transformative education to hundreds of students. We are known for our innovative curriculum, award-winning teachers, high impact researchers, and state-of-the-art facilities. Strong industry partnerships enable us to provide diverse high quality practice education experiences to create confident, safe, and practice-ready graduates.",
      "department": "School of Health Sciences and Social Work"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000141838334": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "The Griffith Centre for Mental Health is a leading research centre dedicated to improving mental health outcomes through evidence-based research, clinical innovation, and strong partnerships with health services and communities. This exciting opportunity sits within nationally significant research programs focused on improving access to effective treatments for children and young people experiencing obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD), including major projects funded by the Medical Research Future Fund (MRFF) and the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC). We are seeking a highly motivated Research Fellow (Grade 1) to join the Centre for Mental Health and contribute to the delivery of two major nationally funded clinical research programs focused on child and adolescent OCD. The successful candidate will play a key role in supporting research delivery, project coordination, intervention and assessment activities, and the development of high-impact research outputs. Here’s how you’ll make it matter: Contribute to the delivery of research objectives across the Centre's major mental health research projects. Coordinate research activities, assessments, and intervention delivery within clinical trials. Prepare publications, presentations, and research reports arising from project outcomes. Develop a strong publication record in high-quality, peer-reviewed journals and contribute to competitive grant applications. Assist in the supervision and mentoring of Honours and Higher Degree Research students. Supervise Research Assistants involved in trial activities. Ensure compliance with ethical, safety, and university policies and procedures. Champion professionalism, integrity, and collaboration in line with the University's values and Code of Conduct. This is a full time, fixed term position until August 2029 primarily based at Griffith University Gold Coast campus. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be Research Fellow Grade 1 range $91,705 - $107,420 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $107,295 - $125,682 per annum. \n\n What matters most is your drive to create a brighter future that benefits all. Through our core principles of excellence, ethics and engagement, you’ll thrive on seeing the direct and lasting impact you’ll have on every community and every future. You will also have: PhD in Psychology or an equivalent qualification and/or relevant professional experience. Demonstrated experience in child and adolescent mental health research and practice. Experience delivering evidence-based psychological assessments and interventions. Registration or Provisional Registration as a Psychologist with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA). Strong project management, organisational, and communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively within multidisciplinary research teams. Demonstrated commitment to research excellence and publication outcomes. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Lara Farrell, Director, Griffith Centre of Mental Health at l.farrell@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Monday, 21 September at 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Griffith Health"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000140590059": {
      "title": "First Peoples Employment Talent Network ",
      "description": "The First Peoples Employment Talent Network provides an opportunity to express your interest in working at Griffith and connect with future employment opportunities across our campuses in both professional and academic areas.  When you apply to join the network, your experience, skills and transferable capabilities will be reviewed to ensure they reflect Griffith's workforce needs. If your application aligns, our First Peoples Employment Partner will contact you to discuss your application.  As a member of the Talent Network, you will be notified of future opportunities that may arise across Griffith's campuses at Brisbane South (Nathan), Gold Coast, Logan and South Bank.  To be considered for this network, candidates should be an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   \n\n Why Join Griffith?  \nAt Griffith, we recognise that employment is about more than a job. It's about belonging, growth, connection and opportunity.  Our  First Peoples Employment Action Plan  is focused on creating the conditions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander employees to flourish through cultural safety, leadership development, professional growth, meaningful career pathways and strong community connections. We are committed to ensuring First Peoples staff feel valued, respected and empowered to achieve their career aspirations.   When you join Griffith, you'll become part of a values-led organisation guided by excellence, inclusion, integrity, innovation and reciprocity.    We offer:  A culturally safe and inclusive workplace that values Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people and cultures.  Opportunities to connect with other First Peoples staff through dedicated staff networks and community-building initiatives.   Leadership, learning and professional development opportunities designed to support career progression and long-term success.   Flexible work arrangements, including a mix of work-from-home and on-campus options.  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts.  Generous leave provisions, including paid First Peoples Cultural leave, paid parental leave and leave loading.  Support for future study and professional development through Griffith's Educational Staff Assistance Scheme.  Opportunities to contribute to Indigenous-led research, education and community engagement initiatives that create meaningful impact.   You may be an experienced professional, academic, researcher, recent graduate or someone looking to bring your transferable skills into the higher education sector. Through the First Peoples Employment Talent Network, Griffith seeks to build relationships with talented Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people who are interested in contributing to a university that values Indigenous leadership, knowledge and excellence.   Griffith University values equity, diversity and inclusion. For more information, please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. If you require any accessibility adjustments to submit your application, please contact us. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  \n \n How to apply  \nPlease submit your application online and ensure your application includes the following:  Current curriculum vitae, including name and contact details.  Cover Letter which should be no more than 2 pages in length.  The First Peoples Employment Talent Network is only open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants - people who:   a) are of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; and   \nb) identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and   \nc) are accepted by their community as being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   Further Information  \nFor further information about the First Peoples Talent Network visit  griffith.edu.au/equity/first-peoples-employment  or contact Bronwyn Horn, First Peoples Employment Partner at  b.horn@griffith.edu.au . You can also stay up to date with all recruitment activity at Griffith via our  First Peoples Employment at Griffith LinkedIn  group.   \n\n Join Griffith University and be part of a community where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are supported to flourish.  At Griffith, we are committed to creating the conditions for First Peoples staff to thrive. We aspire to be the university of choice for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples by fostering a culturally safe workplace, supporting career growth and leadership, celebrating Indigenous knowledges, and creating meaningful employment opportunities across the University.",
      "department": "Corporate Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000140589949": {
      "title": "First Peoples Employment Talent Network",
      "description": "The First Peoples Employment Talent Network provides an opportunity to express your interest in working at Griffith and connect with future employment opportunities across our campuses in both professional and academic areas.  When you apply to join the network, your experience, skills and transferable capabilities will be reviewed to ensure they reflect Griffith's workforce needs. If your application aligns, our First Peoples Employment Partner will contact you to discuss your application.  As a member of the Talent Network, you will be notified of future opportunities that may arise across Griffith's campuses at Brisbane South (Nathan), Gold Coast, Logan and South Bank.  To be considered for this network, candidates should be an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   \n\n Why Join Griffith?  \nAt Griffith, we recognise that employment is about more than a job. It's about belonging, growth, connection and opportunity.  Our  First Peoples Employment Action Plan  is focused on creating the conditions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander employees to flourish through cultural safety, leadership development, professional growth, meaningful career pathways and strong community connections. We are committed to ensuring First Peoples staff feel valued, respected and empowered to achieve their career aspirations.   When you join Griffith, you'll become part of a values-led organisation guided by excellence, inclusion, integrity, innovation and reciprocity.    We offer:  A culturally safe and inclusive workplace that values Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people and cultures.  Opportunities to connect with other First Peoples staff through dedicated staff networks and community-building initiatives.   Leadership, learning and professional development opportunities designed to support career progression and long-term success.   Flexible work arrangements, including a mix of work-from-home and on-campus options.  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts.  Generous leave provisions, including paid First Peoples Cultural leave, paid parental leave and leave loading.  Support for future study and professional development through Griffith's Educational Staff Assistance Scheme.  Opportunities to contribute to Indigenous-led research, education and community engagement initiatives that create meaningful impact.   You may be an experienced professional, academic, researcher, recent graduate or someone looking to bring your transferable skills into the higher education sector. Through the First Peoples Employment Talent Network, Griffith seeks to build relationships with talented Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people who are interested in contributing to a university that values Indigenous leadership, knowledge and excellence.   Griffith University values equity, diversity and inclusion. For more information, please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. If you require any accessibility adjustments to submit your application, please contact us. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  \n \n How to apply  \nPlease submit your application online and ensure your application includes the following:  Current curriculum vitae, including name and contact details.  Cover Letter which should be no more than 2 pages in length.  The First Peoples Employment Talent Network is only open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants - people who:   a) are of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; and   \nb) identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and   \nc) are accepted by their community as being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   Further Information  \nFor further information about the First Peoples Talent Network visit  griffith.edu.au/equity/first-peoples-employment  or contact Bronwyn Horn, First Peoples Employment Partner at  b.horn@griffith.edu.au . You can also stay up to date with all recruitment activity at Griffith via our  First Peoples Employment at Griffith LinkedIn  group.   \n\n Join Griffith University and be part of a community where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are supported to flourish.  At Griffith, we are committed to creating the conditions for First Peoples staff to thrive. We aspire to be the university of choice for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples by fostering a culturally safe workplace, supporting career growth and leadership, celebrating Indigenous knowledges, and creating meaningful employment opportunities across the University.",
      "department": "Corporate Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000140111718": {
      "title": "First Peoples Employment Talent Network",
      "description": "The First Peoples Employment Talent Network provides an opportunity to express your interest in working at Griffith and connect with future employment opportunities across our campuses in both professional and academic areas.  When you apply to join the network, your experience, skills and transferable capabilities will be reviewed to ensure they reflect Griffith's workforce needs. If your application aligns, our First Peoples Employment Partner will contact you to discuss your application.  As a member of the Talent Network, you will be notified of future opportunities that may arise across Griffith's campuses at Brisbane South (Nathan), Gold Coast, Logan and South Bank.  To be considered for this network, candidates should be an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   \n\n Why Join Griffith?  \nAt Griffith, we recognise that employment is about more than a job. It's about belonging, growth, connection and opportunity.  Our  First Peoples Employment Action Plan  is focused on creating the conditions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander employees to flourish through cultural safety, leadership development, professional growth, meaningful career pathways and strong community connections. We are committed to ensuring First Peoples staff feel valued, respected and empowered to achieve their career aspirations.   When you join Griffith, you'll become part of a values-led organisation guided by excellence, inclusion, integrity, innovation and reciprocity.    We offer:  A culturally safe and inclusive workplace that values Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people and cultures.  Opportunities to connect with other First Peoples staff through dedicated staff networks and community-building initiatives.   Leadership, learning and professional development opportunities designed to support career progression and long-term success.   Flexible work arrangements, including a mix of work-from-home and on-campus options.  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts.  Generous leave provisions, including paid First Peoples Cultural leave, paid parental leave and leave loading.  Support for future study and professional development through Griffith's Educational Staff Assistance Scheme.  Opportunities to contribute to Indigenous-led research, education and community engagement initiatives that create meaningful impact.   You may be an experienced professional, academic, researcher, recent graduate or someone looking to bring your transferable skills into the higher education sector. Through the First Peoples Employment Talent Network, Griffith seeks to build relationships with talented Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people who are interested in contributing to a university that values Indigenous leadership, knowledge and excellence.   Griffith University values equity, diversity and inclusion. For more information, please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. If you require any accessibility adjustments to submit your application, please contact us. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  \n \n How to apply  \nPlease submit your application online and ensure your application includes the following:  Current curriculum vitae, including name and contact details.  Cover Letter which should be no more than 2 pages in length.  The First Peoples Employment Talent Network is only open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants - people who:   a) are of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; and   \nb) identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and   \nc) are accepted by their community as being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   Further Information  \nFor further information about the First Peoples Talent Network visit  griffith.edu.au/equity/first-peoples-employment  or contact Bronwyn Horn, First Peoples Employment Partner at  b.horn@griffith.edu.au . You can also stay up to date with all recruitment activity at Griffith via our  First Peoples Employment at Griffith LinkedIn  group.   \n\n Join Griffith University and be part of a community where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are supported to flourish.  At Griffith, we are committed to creating the conditions for First Peoples staff to thrive. We aspire to be the university of choice for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples by fostering a culturally safe workplace, supporting career growth and leadership, celebrating Indigenous knowledges, and creating meaningful employment opportunities across the University.",
      "department": "Corporate Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000120831317": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarships: Machine Vision",
      "description": "This research aims to advance knowledge of machine vision for deep visual understanding of natural objects and environments. The primary focus of this role is to undertake fundamental research in visual understanding and multimodal machine learning. Research activities include developing novel deep learning methods for representation learning, vision-language reasoning, fine-grained and ultra-fine-grained visual categorization, and trustworthy AI systems. The candidate will publish high-quality research papers in leading conferences and journals in computer vision and machine learning. Successful candidates will have the opportunity to receive advanced research training and work within a vibrant research environment that includes a team of research fellows, industry collaborators, and PhD students. The position provides opportunities for collaboration with leading researchers and participation in international research communities in computer vision, machine learning, and artificial intelligence. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of academic merit and research background. To be successful in this role, you should demonstrate strong analytical and problem-solving skills, solid mathematical foundation, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a research environment. Evidence of a passion for computer vision and image processing is essential. Previous experiences in deep learning, visual recognition, multimodal AI, pattern recognition, or data-driven modelling are preferred. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and international applicants. Applicants must have completed a Bachelor’s degree with honours equivalent to  first-class honours , or a Master’s degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a Bachelor honours degree, or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Policy . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program, refer  here . Please note : Applicants with academic qualifications in computer science, engineering, applied mathematics, with a publication record in top tier venues such as CVPR, ICCV, ECCV, NeurIPS, AAAI, IJCAI are preferred. International applicants should ensure that English Language Proficiency requirements for the program are met before formally applying. Applicants to research programs will need to show they meet: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR A score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 OR A score of no less than 3+ in each skill of the ISLPR (conducted by ISLPR Language Services only) OR A minimum overall score of 176 (no score less than 169) on C1 Advanced  or  C2 Proficiency OR An overall score of 58 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 50 (applies to tests taken prior to 7 August 2025) OR An overall score of 55 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 47 (applies to tests taken on or after 7 August 2025) OR An overall score of 70 in the LanguageCert Academic OR An overall score of 59 in the Michigan English Test (MET) . \n\n About the scholarship \nThe successful candidate will be awarded a Griffith University Postgraduate Research Scholarship (GUPRS) or an equivalent scholarship package funded through the ARC Industry Laureate Fellowship. \n \nThe 2026 Griffith University Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $35,656 (indexed) for a period of up to three years of full-time study. Please see the GUPRS Conditions of Award for more information. A successful International applicant will also be awarded a Griffith University International Postgraduate Research Scholarship to cover tuition fees for up to three years. Please see the GUIPRS Conditions of Award for more information. \n \n EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to yongsheng.gao@griffith.edu.au, containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Brief research proposal, maximum 2 pages Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is: Currently Open  The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line \n \n What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nProfessor Yongsheng Gao \nEmail:  yongsheng.gao@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n We are currently looking for 4 highly motivated PhD candidates to join the research of “Next-Generation Visual Understanding” funded by the ARC Industry Laureate Fellowship. The successful candidate will join the research team led by an Australian Industry Laureate Fellow and internationally recognized researchers in smart farming, computer vision, machine learning, and artificial intelligence, within the School of Engineering and Built Environment at Griffith University. The research environment is collaborative and multidisciplinary, combining expertise in deep learning, computer vision, data science, and applied AI, and working closely with industry partners, national research institutions, and international collaborators. \n \nThis opportunity will be primarily based at the Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000071420565": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship:  Validate novel salivary biomarkers for the early detection of liver fibrosis",
      "description": "We are currently seeking highly motivated PhD candidates to join our research team at Griffith University. This exciting project aims to develop and validate novel salivary biomarkers for the early detection of liver fibrosis. Highlights of working on this project include: Focus on non-invasive, saliva-based diagnostics for liver fibrosis. Integration of clinical collaboration with hospital partners and cutting-edge research in the laboratory. Opportunity to work at the intersection of translational medicine, proteomics, and biomarker development. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful in this role, you should have: A strong academic background in Medical Laboratory Science, Pathology, Molecular Pathology, or a closely related field. Demonstrated research experience working at the interface with hospital laboratories and working with clinicians. Evidence of collaborative skills and the ability to work effectively with clinical and laboratory-based research teams. A clear passion for research and a commitment to advancing the field of liver fibrosis paving the way for precision health. Should hold a valid Australian driver's licence. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to  first  class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Procedure .  For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . Please note : Applicants must have obtained a Master’s degree or an Honours degree in relative fields. A solid background in Medical Laboratory Science, Pathology, Molecular Pathology with an interest in biomarker discovery is required. Some experience in the field of multivariate statistical analysis would be beneficial but is not required. International applicants should ensure that English Language Proficiency requirements for the program are met before formally applying. Applicants to research programs will need to show they meet: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR A minimum score of 575 on the paper-based TOEFL including a score of no less than 5.0 on the TWE OR A score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 \n\n About the scholarship \nThe 2025 Griffith University Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $34,752 (indexed) for a period of up to three years of full-time study. Please see the  GUPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. A successful International applicant will also be awarded a Griffith University International Postgraduate Research Scholarship to cover tuition fees for up to three years. Please see the  GUIPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. \n What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  c.punyadeera@griffith.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Research proposal Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the about you section, including a  copy of your academic transcript A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names of  two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Tuesday 29 July 2025 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nProfessor Chamindie Punyadeera \nEmail: c.punyadeera@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The Saliva and Liquid Biopsy Group is dedicated to advancing biomarker discovery for metabolic diseases and cancer, with the goal of improving precision health. Our research focuses on identifying and validating biomarkers that enable more accurate patient stratification based on disease risk, prognosis, and treatment response. The successful PhD applicant will be based at the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics (IBG), Griffith University, Brisbane South (Nathan) and will work within a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000040674965": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship:  Synthetic biopolymer-protein conjugates for enhanced mucosal delivery of drugs and antigens",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to develop innovative biopolymer-protein conjugates for enhanced mucosal delivery of drugs and antigens. This will include electrohydrodynamic atomisation for nasal delivery. The primary focus of this role is to design and develop functional biopolymers that target the mucosa and that are amendable toward electrohydrodynamic atomisation to overcome the current drug and antigen delivery limitations. The project aims to enhance the efficacy of biopolymer micro/nanoparticles through precise control of particle size and charge and fill the gap between the fast advancement in nasal drug and vaccine discovery and conventional ineffective delivery techniques. Success in this role requires knowledge in design of functional polymer particles, generation of cell factories as production strains, bioprocess development and characterisation of nanomaterials before and after the nebulising process. \n  \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful within this role, you will be able to work in bioengineering, microfluidics, and material science. You will have evidence of a passion for conducting high impact research, with strong desire to undertake research in multidisciplinary research. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to  first  class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Procedure .  For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . Please note : You should have strong background in Material/Biomaterial science and experiences in design, analysis, fabrication and characterization of new material samples. International applicants should ensure that English Language Proficiency requirements for the program are met before formally applying. Applicants to research programs will need to show they meet: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR A minimum score of 575 on the paper-based TOEFL including a score of no less than 5.0 on the TWE OR A score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 \n  \n\n About the scholarship \nThe 2025 Griffith University Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $34,752 (indexed) for a period of up to three years of full-time study. Please see the  GUPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. A successful International applicant will also be awarded a Griffith University International Postgraduate Research Scholarship to cover tuition fees for up to three years. Please see the  GUIPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. \n \n EoI process \nPlease do not  select the “I'm Interested” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  v.dau@griffith.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Research proposal Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the about you section, including a  copy of your academic transcript A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names of two referees Closing date of expressions of interest: Friday 7 March 2025 at 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship online. \n \n What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across locations in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nDr Van Dau \nEmail:  v.dau@griffith.edu.au \n \nProfessor Bernd Rehm \nEmail:  b.rehm@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n Working for one of the most influential universities in Australia and the Asia–Pacific region, you will join a University that spans five physical locations in South East Queensland and all disciplines, while our network of more than 200,000 graduates extends worldwide. We have world-class facilities for micro- and nanotechnology and advanced manufacturing. You will conduct the advanced research at the Centre for Cell Factories and Biopolymers, the centre equipped with modern bioprocessing equipment, including cell disruptors, homogenisers, bioreactors, and chromatography system. It is also located in a PC2 laboratory which is equipped with an array of instruments that enable synthetic biology of bacterial production strain. This opportunity will be primarily based at Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    }
  },
